WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.690 --> 00:00:07.060
&gt;&gt; I'm very pleased to
welcome Adam Chlipala

00:00:07.061 --> 00:00:10.550
from MIT today and his
two students as well,

00:00:10.551 --> 00:00:13.170
Ben and Clemar, who
are here, visiting.

00:00:13.171 --> 00:00:15.435
Adam is going to tell us about

00:00:15.436 --> 00:00:19.540
Fiat Crypto which is a way to

00:00:19.541 --> 00:00:23.154
produce efficient low
level cryptographic code,

00:00:23.155 --> 00:00:26.618
sort of like construction
from high level specs.

00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:29.125
Looking forward to
hearing about it.

00:00:29.126 --> 00:00:31.120
&gt;&gt; Okay, thanks. And let me say,

00:00:31.121 --> 00:00:34.060
I'm very glad to have
questions throughout the talk,

00:00:34.061 --> 00:00:35.275
not just at the end.

00:00:35.276 --> 00:00:38.455
So, let's deal with
this Fiat Cryptography stuff.

00:00:38.456 --> 00:00:41.380
Well, I'll give
the initial setting here,

00:00:41.381 --> 00:00:43.120
which I'm sure many
of you know working

00:00:43.121 --> 00:00:45.225
on very similar things.

00:00:45.226 --> 00:00:47.020
But let me just give

00:00:47.021 --> 00:00:49.660
the cartoon picture
of what it is to use

00:00:49.661 --> 00:00:55.540
the TLS protocol to initiate
an HTTPS web connection.

00:00:55.541 --> 00:00:58.615
So, here's the web browser,
there's the web server.

00:00:58.616 --> 00:01:00.970
Web server has a public key
and a certificate

00:01:00.971 --> 00:01:03.595
that have been signed by
some authority to tell you.

00:01:03.596 --> 00:01:05.335
Whoever knows that private key

00:01:05.336 --> 00:01:07.570
really is the company
they claim to be.

00:01:07.571 --> 00:01:09.190
So, the web browser
wants to initiate

00:01:09.191 --> 00:01:11.475
a connection with
the web server.

00:01:11.476 --> 00:01:13.320
So, a key exchange protocol

00:01:13.321 --> 00:01:15.180
is used to establish
some secret that

00:01:15.181 --> 00:01:19.375
only these two parties know.
That is a symmetric key.

00:01:19.376 --> 00:01:21.970
And then, we need to check that,

00:01:21.971 --> 00:01:23.075
the browser needs to check it's

00:01:23.076 --> 00:01:24.880
talking to the server
it meant to talk to.

00:01:24.881 --> 00:01:27.535
So, the certificate and

00:01:27.536 --> 00:01:29.920
public key are retrieved
and then we use

00:01:29.921 --> 00:01:33.100
a digital signature algorithm
for the server to prove

00:01:33.101 --> 00:01:35.620
that the party you

00:01:35.621 --> 00:01:37.450
were just talking to to
learn that symmetric key,

00:01:37.451 --> 00:01:38.590
well, it must be

00:01:38.591 --> 00:01:41.425
the following company
that you were talking to.

00:01:41.426 --> 00:01:42.700
And now, from that point on,

00:01:42.701 --> 00:01:44.710
we can just use
symmetric key crypto,

00:01:44.711 --> 00:01:48.615
which is generally a lot
cheaper than public key crypto.

00:01:48.616 --> 00:01:52.390
So, every single HTTPS
connection that goes

00:01:52.391 --> 00:01:53.830
on needs to go through

00:01:53.831 --> 00:01:57.305
this little dance to
establish the symmetric key.

00:01:57.306 --> 00:01:59.080
The first two stages
of that process that

00:01:59.081 --> 00:02:01.165
use public key cryptography,

00:02:01.166 --> 00:02:03.430
they only run once per
session but there can be

00:02:03.431 --> 00:02:06.970
many HTTPS connections coming
into a serious datacenter.

00:02:06.971 --> 00:02:08.410
So, it's important to optimize

00:02:08.411 --> 00:02:10.925
the performance of
the public key part as well.

00:02:10.926 --> 00:02:13.060
And the challenges
there have to do

00:02:13.061 --> 00:02:16.180
with trading off between
correctness and performance.

00:02:16.181 --> 00:02:18.490
And, in fact,

00:02:18.491 --> 00:02:20.890
the public key algorithms
in practice are a lot harder

00:02:20.891 --> 00:02:23.020
to implement correctly and prove

00:02:23.021 --> 00:02:25.920
correct than the
private key algorithms.

00:02:25.921 --> 00:02:28.135
And one of the biggest
challenges here

00:02:28.136 --> 00:02:31.000
is doing big integer arithmetic,

00:02:31.001 --> 00:02:33.820
modulus some huge prime number,

00:02:33.821 --> 00:02:36.400
and that is what
we're going to try

00:02:36.401 --> 00:02:39.670
to get right with
programming tool support.

00:02:39.671 --> 00:02:42.560
So, how bad can it be to
do arithmetic, after all?

00:02:42.561 --> 00:02:45.940
Well, there are not

00:02:45.941 --> 00:02:48.010
that many different crypto
libraries used widely,

00:02:48.011 --> 00:02:50.135
luckily, and they're built
by pretty competent people.

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:51.340
But they really have their work

00:02:51.341 --> 00:02:52.475
cut out for them because there's

00:02:52.476 --> 00:02:53.770
a whole space of algorithms that

00:02:53.771 --> 00:02:56.410
matter in practice
for cryptography.

00:02:56.411 --> 00:02:58.400
There's another space of

00:02:58.401 --> 00:03:00.230
different prime moduli that

00:03:00.231 --> 00:03:02.270
matter for implementing
these algorithms.

00:03:02.271 --> 00:03:03.320
And then they're, as usual,

00:03:03.321 --> 00:03:05.840
they're a bunch of different
hardware architectures.

00:03:05.841 --> 00:03:07.830
It is surprisingly
close in practice.

00:03:07.831 --> 00:03:10.940
It's true when I say
that every element of

00:03:10.941 --> 00:03:13.220
this three-way
Cartesian product gets

00:03:13.221 --> 00:03:16.265
implemented separately
with no code sharing.

00:03:16.266 --> 00:03:19.760
And only a relatively small set
of experts on the planet

00:03:19.761 --> 00:03:20.935
know how to do it in a way

00:03:20.936 --> 00:03:23.325
that achieves
acceptable performance.

00:03:23.326 --> 00:03:26.135
So, when you change the prime
number, it really is true,

00:03:26.136 --> 00:03:28.790
the experts rewrite
the assembly code from scratch

00:03:28.791 --> 00:03:30.290
for every hardware architecture

00:03:30.291 --> 00:03:31.768
to get the best performance.

00:03:31.769 --> 00:03:33.740
This is the state of practice.

00:03:33.741 --> 00:03:37.950
And they get it wrong
a decent fraction of the time.

00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:42.680
We found quite a few
bug tracker issues that

00:03:42.681 --> 00:03:44.780
were related to
getting this kind of

00:03:44.781 --> 00:03:47.570
arithmetic wrong for
different kinds of protocols.

00:03:47.571 --> 00:03:49.415
So, there's a real
opportunity here to

00:03:49.416 --> 00:03:53.650
improve the developing
tool story for this space.

00:03:53.651 --> 00:03:56.812
Here's an example of someone
getting something wrong.

00:03:56.813 --> 00:03:59.425
This was a comment in

00:03:59.426 --> 00:04:02.290
reference C code written
by the same people

00:04:02.291 --> 00:04:05.305
who designed the primitive
that it's implementing.

00:04:05.306 --> 00:04:06.700
They left a comment saying,

00:04:06.701 --> 00:04:08.410
I'm not really sure
if this variable

00:04:08.411 --> 00:04:10.385
can be negative or not.

00:04:10.386 --> 00:04:12.220
You should probably
go read this book,

00:04:12.221 --> 00:04:16.065
and if you figure out
that it could be negative,

00:04:16.066 --> 00:04:17.855
let us know, fix it here.

00:04:17.856 --> 00:04:21.520
So, that's not exactly
confidence enhancing.

00:04:21.521 --> 00:04:24.430
And it comes from
this very cleverly named

00:04:24.431 --> 00:04:27.250
online benchmarking
comparison site

00:04:27.251 --> 00:04:29.105
called SUPERCOP that has this,

00:04:29.106 --> 00:04:30.415
of course, completely rational

00:04:30.416 --> 00:04:31.780
expansion down
here at the bottom.

00:04:31.781 --> 00:04:32.860
This is what everyone uses for

00:04:32.861 --> 00:04:35.335
benchmarking cryptography code.

00:04:35.336 --> 00:04:38.660
Another bug, by
the same people again,

00:04:38.661 --> 00:04:39.880
with the reference
implementation,

00:04:39.881 --> 00:04:41.775
essentially of their own
algorithms they invented,

00:04:41.776 --> 00:04:44.710
was they read about
10,000 lines of

00:04:44.711 --> 00:04:46.020
assembly code manually to get

00:04:46.021 --> 00:04:48.020
the expected level
of performance.

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:52.480
And oops, they wrote R1 when
they meant R2 in one place,

00:04:52.481 --> 00:04:55.995
and this meant that
the code was incorrect.

00:04:55.996 --> 00:04:59.115
There was also a bug

00:04:59.116 --> 00:05:01.560
in open SSL that involved
a disagreement about

00:05:01.561 --> 00:05:04.295
the contract between
the caller and callee of

00:05:04.296 --> 00:05:08.005
a divide with remainder
kind of operation.

00:05:08.006 --> 00:05:13.950
And oops, the people
who found and analyzed

00:05:13.951 --> 00:05:16.875
this bug computed that

00:05:16.876 --> 00:05:20.445
using at least the
dumb random testing,

00:05:20.446 --> 00:05:22.190
the chances of finding
the bugs were something

00:05:22.191 --> 00:05:24.990
like tends the two
to negative 29.

00:05:24.991 --> 00:05:26.430
There are a lot of corner cases

00:05:26.431 --> 00:05:27.860
to worry about in
this kind of code,

00:05:27.861 --> 00:05:29.250
not always obvious
what they are.

00:05:29.251 --> 00:05:32.250
So, testing is not
super effective at

00:05:32.251 --> 00:05:33.550
catching problems that can

00:05:33.551 --> 00:05:35.520
have serious security
consequences.

00:05:35.521 --> 00:05:37.975
So, let's try to do better.

00:05:37.976 --> 00:05:40.355
Our project is
Fiat Cryptography.

00:05:40.356 --> 00:05:42.570
Here's a cool logo made by

00:05:42.571 --> 00:05:44.010
professional designer
in the context

00:05:44.011 --> 00:05:45.270
of our larger project,

00:05:45.271 --> 00:05:46.740
Science of Deep Specification,

00:05:46.741 --> 00:05:49.360
which is a multi-university
initiative.

00:05:49.361 --> 00:05:50.940
I don't know how many people
recognize the spy

00:05:50.941 --> 00:05:52.915
versus spy iconography here,

00:05:52.916 --> 00:05:55.215
it's a classic comic strip and

00:05:55.216 --> 00:05:56.745
the spy has become a rooster

00:05:56.746 --> 00:05:58.680
because we use
the cock proof assistant,

00:05:58.681 --> 00:06:00.805
so it's a natural match.

00:06:00.806 --> 00:06:03.000
So, what is this project?

00:06:03.001 --> 00:06:06.690
Well, let me tell
the general story of how

00:06:06.691 --> 00:06:08.745
Correct-by-Construction
Cryptography might work

00:06:08.746 --> 00:06:10.960
and then tell you which parts
this project deals with.

00:06:10.961 --> 00:06:13.875
So, let's say we have
some abstract security property

00:06:13.876 --> 00:06:16.115
like if you're able to

00:06:16.116 --> 00:06:17.400
produce a signature fast

00:06:17.401 --> 00:06:20.180
enough then you must have
known the secret key.

00:06:20.181 --> 00:06:23.190
We might do protocol
verification to show that

00:06:23.191 --> 00:06:25.410
a particular mathematical
algorithm written at

00:06:25.411 --> 00:06:27.210
a relatively high level

00:06:27.211 --> 00:06:29.640
truly provides
that security property.

00:06:29.641 --> 00:06:32.610
And we might do
implementation verification

00:06:32.611 --> 00:06:33.780
or synthesis to show that

00:06:33.781 --> 00:06:36.195
the actual performant
low level code

00:06:36.196 --> 00:06:39.060
is a correct realization of
that mathematical algorithm.

00:06:39.061 --> 00:06:42.645
This project is only
the second of these two steps,

00:06:42.646 --> 00:06:44.100
though we'd be
very interested in

00:06:44.101 --> 00:06:45.420
connecting to results of

00:06:45.421 --> 00:06:46.500
the first kind and we're doing

00:06:46.501 --> 00:06:48.965
some preliminary work
along those lines now.

00:06:48.966 --> 00:06:51.840
So, let me zoom in
on the second part.

00:06:51.841 --> 00:06:53.925
So, we have a
mathematical algorithm.

00:06:53.926 --> 00:06:55.935
We're working in particular

00:06:55.936 --> 00:06:58.365
on something called
elliptic curve cryptography,

00:06:58.366 --> 00:06:59.700
which is what's
typically used for

00:06:59.701 --> 00:07:01.740
those first stages of
TLS that I talked about.

00:07:01.741 --> 00:07:03.540
So, we're in
algebraic structures

00:07:03.541 --> 00:07:06.060
with two dimensional points
to start out with.

00:07:06.061 --> 00:07:10.045
And it turns out,
that in practice,

00:07:10.046 --> 00:07:12.990
two dimensional points are
implemented with points in

00:07:12.991 --> 00:07:15.930
more dimensions because it is

00:07:15.931 --> 00:07:17.550
faster and helps you
avoid the process

00:07:17.551 --> 00:07:19.635
or instructions that
are the slow ones to

00:07:19.636 --> 00:07:21.780
introduce extra points in a way

00:07:21.781 --> 00:07:23.280
that is largely orthogonal to

00:07:23.281 --> 00:07:24.675
what I'll be mostly
talking about,

00:07:24.676 --> 00:07:26.865
so I won't say
more about it here.

00:07:26.866 --> 00:07:30.720
But then we have basically
two functional programs,

00:07:30.721 --> 00:07:33.600
one that uses
the simple representation,

00:07:33.601 --> 00:07:35.640
the other that uses
the fancy representation.

00:07:35.641 --> 00:07:38.940
And we prove that
the second one is

00:07:38.941 --> 00:07:40.230
a correct implementation of

00:07:40.231 --> 00:07:44.060
the abstract data type that
the the first one defines.

00:07:44.061 --> 00:07:45.450
Then, we need to take

00:07:45.451 --> 00:07:47.385
the individual coordinates
of these points,

00:07:47.386 --> 00:07:51.050
which are big integers
in a modular prime field,

00:07:51.051 --> 00:07:52.170
and we need to show that

00:07:52.171 --> 00:07:53.780
we're correctly
implementing integers,

00:07:53.781 --> 00:07:55.290
so that they're so
big, they don't fit

00:07:55.291 --> 00:07:57.545
in single hardware registers.

00:07:57.546 --> 00:07:58.710
We need to divide

00:07:58.711 --> 00:08:00.690
one number across
multiple registers and

00:08:00.691 --> 00:08:04.285
that's really the heart
of what we're doing here.

00:08:04.286 --> 00:08:06.690
So, we prove an
abstraction relation about

00:08:06.691 --> 00:08:10.050
the implementation of single
integers within the points

00:08:10.051 --> 00:08:16.670
as multiple roughly
machine word sized pieces.

00:08:16.671 --> 00:08:19.435
And then we take

00:08:19.436 --> 00:08:22.180
particular choices of how

00:08:22.181 --> 00:08:24.490
to split a number into
multiple parts and then

00:08:24.491 --> 00:08:25.900
we specialize our library of

00:08:25.901 --> 00:08:28.510
code to the particular
choices you've made to

00:08:28.511 --> 00:08:31.435
get idiomatic C-like code

00:08:31.436 --> 00:08:34.300
that is customized
to those choices.

00:08:34.301 --> 00:08:35.530
And we also do

00:08:35.531 --> 00:08:37.935
abstract interpretation
to infer bit widths

00:08:37.936 --> 00:08:41.170
of temporaries and assign them

00:08:41.171 --> 00:08:45.370
to the appropriately sized
registers and local variables.

00:08:45.371 --> 00:08:46.600
And our compiler for

00:08:46.601 --> 00:08:49.250
that last part is proved
correct once and for all,

00:08:49.251 --> 00:08:50.755
whereas,

00:08:50.756 --> 00:08:52.690
some of the earlier
stages are proof

00:08:52.691 --> 00:08:56.735
generating instead of verified.

00:08:56.736 --> 00:09:01.400
So, several people in

00:09:01.401 --> 00:09:03.506
this room have been doing
very closely related things.

00:09:03.507 --> 00:09:05.090
So, let me give
the comparison with

00:09:05.091 --> 00:09:09.270
the most closely related
projects that I know about.

00:09:09.271 --> 00:09:12.335
So, there's the HACL*
project which is

00:09:12.336 --> 00:09:16.175
an F* based crypto
library using these Low*,

00:09:16.176 --> 00:09:18.440
Vale, which Chris
has been working on

00:09:18.441 --> 00:09:20.300
doing metaprogramming to build

00:09:20.301 --> 00:09:21.950
this kind of code and

00:09:21.951 --> 00:09:24.240
reason about that code,
generators using Daphne.

00:09:24.241 --> 00:09:26.600
And there's a project called
Jasmin which is, basically,

00:09:26.601 --> 00:09:27.800
a verified compiler for

00:09:27.801 --> 00:09:28.820
a low level language that is

00:09:28.821 --> 00:09:30.665
appropriate for cryptography.

00:09:30.666 --> 00:09:33.530
So, quick summary of

00:09:33.531 --> 00:09:34.790
the advantages of those projects

00:09:34.791 --> 00:09:36.585
over what I'm
talking about here.

00:09:36.586 --> 00:09:38.210
I've showed you
a few steps and said

00:09:38.211 --> 00:09:39.965
we only do some of them.

00:09:39.966 --> 00:09:41.060
Well, these other projects are

00:09:41.061 --> 00:09:42.320
doing more of the other steps,

00:09:42.321 --> 00:09:43.955
at least as I understand things,

00:09:43.956 --> 00:09:45.950
and their results are pushed all

00:09:45.951 --> 00:09:48.300
the way down to
assembly code in some cases.

00:09:48.301 --> 00:09:50.360
And we are currently stopping

00:09:50.361 --> 00:09:52.880
at a higher level,
roughly C-code level.

00:09:52.881 --> 00:09:54.950
And also, the project I'll be

00:09:54.951 --> 00:09:57.140
presenting finishes
up generating

00:09:57.141 --> 00:09:58.355
just straight line code,

00:09:58.356 --> 00:09:59.950
no control flow and
these others all

00:09:59.951 --> 00:10:02.720
have stories for control flow.

00:10:02.721 --> 00:10:05.150
Some of the pros of

00:10:05.151 --> 00:10:07.235
our approach are that

00:10:07.236 --> 00:10:09.048
we have a much smaller
trusted code base.

00:10:09.049 --> 00:10:11.210
We're doing the usual cock thing
where we don't have to

00:10:11.211 --> 00:10:14.765
trust SMT solvers or
compilers or any of that.

00:10:14.766 --> 00:10:17.600
And, more fundamentally,

00:10:17.601 --> 00:10:21.120
we are showing
complete automation

00:10:21.121 --> 00:10:22.745
of regenerating a protocol,

00:10:22.746 --> 00:10:23.990
or a primitive actually,

00:10:23.991 --> 00:10:25.850
when you change,

00:10:25.851 --> 00:10:28.580
what is the prime modulus
for your arithmetic.

00:10:28.581 --> 00:10:31.010
It sounds innocuous,
but, in practice,

00:10:31.011 --> 00:10:34.260
this is what implementers
spend most of their time doing.

00:10:34.261 --> 00:10:35.660
When you change
the prime modulus,

00:10:35.661 --> 00:10:36.845
rewrite all the code,

00:10:36.846 --> 00:10:38.660
our compiler does
that automatically.

00:10:38.661 --> 00:10:40.295
And we also regain

00:10:40.296 --> 00:10:43.385
the advantage we're
used to since the 80s.

00:10:43.386 --> 00:10:44.750
We have compilers that retargets

00:10:44.751 --> 00:10:47.930
new hardware architectures
automatically.

00:10:47.931 --> 00:10:49.655
It turns out, and I'll
show an example soon,

00:10:49.656 --> 00:10:51.350
this code is completely
rewritten for

00:10:51.351 --> 00:10:54.020
each new architecture and
the state of practice.

00:10:54.021 --> 00:10:56.150
So, our compiler generalizes
over both those dimensions

00:10:56.151 --> 00:10:57.410
and rebuild stuff for you

00:10:57.411 --> 00:11:00.605
automatically when
you change either one.

00:11:00.606 --> 00:11:03.545
Given that there are
few people in the room

00:11:03.546 --> 00:11:04.790
whose work I'm
comparing against,

00:11:04.791 --> 00:11:08.075
any questions at this point?

00:11:08.076 --> 00:11:13.180
Okay. All right.

00:11:13.181 --> 00:11:15.180
So,

00:11:15.181 --> 00:11:18.180
&gt;&gt; Jasmin guys would really
disagree with you [inaudible]

00:11:18.181 --> 00:11:22.280
&gt;&gt; Good thing we kept them away.

00:11:22.281 --> 00:11:25.655
All right. So, in other words,

00:11:25.656 --> 00:11:29.225
one dimension of variation
in code in this space is

00:11:29.226 --> 00:11:30.720
the different prime moduli

00:11:30.721 --> 00:11:32.465
that we're doing
our arithmetic with.

00:11:32.466 --> 00:11:35.460
Another is all the hardware
architectures out there.

