00:15:43.099 --> 00:15:46.000
So, let me go back and
talk a bit more about

00:15:46.001 --> 00:15:47.570
what really is going
on in this arithmetic

00:15:47.571 --> 00:15:49.590
and what are the challenges.

00:15:49.591 --> 00:15:51.955
So, like I said,

00:15:51.956 --> 00:15:54.790
different prime moduli lead to

00:15:54.791 --> 00:15:57.040
dramatically different
implementation strategies

00:15:57.041 --> 00:15:58.060
to get the best performance

00:15:58.061 --> 00:15:59.665
on the processors that we have.

00:15:59.666 --> 00:16:01.690
One prime number that's very

00:16:01.691 --> 00:16:05.410
important to the 255 minus 19,

00:16:05.411 --> 00:16:06.945
it was designed by

00:16:06.946 --> 00:16:10.150
Krypto implementation experts to

00:16:10.151 --> 00:16:13.130
have good code on
commodity processors.

00:16:13.131 --> 00:16:16.025
It fits a general pattern
that's been given many names,

00:16:16.026 --> 00:16:17.860
one of them is pseudo-mersenne
primes where you

00:16:17.861 --> 00:16:20.690
have power two minus
a small constant.

00:16:20.691 --> 00:16:21.815
Why do we care about this?

00:16:21.816 --> 00:16:23.420
Why is this going to
make the code fast?

00:16:23.421 --> 00:16:25.600
Well, a key operation that we

00:16:25.601 --> 00:16:28.540
need to come up with
is modular reduction

00:16:28.541 --> 00:16:29.625
which is basically the modern

00:16:29.626 --> 00:16:31.150
operator that we're all used

00:16:31.151 --> 00:16:34.060
to from Math classes.

00:16:34.061 --> 00:16:35.995
And the trick is,

00:16:35.996 --> 00:16:37.690
we know a lot of
mod operators are

00:16:37.691 --> 00:16:39.321
built into the hardware,

00:16:39.322 --> 00:16:40.770
if you happen to work with
the power of two the mod

00:16:40.771 --> 00:16:42.235
is very efficient to compute,

00:16:42.236 --> 00:16:44.020
but in Krypto part of
the point is to make

00:16:44.021 --> 00:16:46.495
things certain operations
not efficient to compute.

00:16:46.496 --> 00:16:49.090
So we have a number that's
almost a power of two but

00:16:49.091 --> 00:16:50.440
unfortunately
almost doesn't count

00:16:50.441 --> 00:16:51.830
here if we're getting
good performance.

00:16:51.831 --> 00:16:55.385
So we're going to need to
work to get mod to be fast.

00:16:55.386 --> 00:16:58.375
So, here's an example of how
we might think about this.

00:16:58.376 --> 00:17:01.450
Let's say I just did
multiplication or

00:17:01.451 --> 00:17:03.220
something where the output is

00:17:03.221 --> 00:17:05.740
naturally twice as wide
as the input operands.

00:17:05.741 --> 00:17:09.220
And so, I can take
the output and think of it

00:17:09.221 --> 00:17:13.765
as a higher order word
and a low order word.

00:17:13.766 --> 00:17:15.490
We shift the higher
order word into

00:17:15.491 --> 00:17:18.550
place by multiplying it
by the right power of two.

00:17:18.551 --> 00:17:19.800
Now I want to take this thing,

00:17:19.801 --> 00:17:21.130
to form the modules operation to

00:17:21.131 --> 00:17:23.605
get it back down
to a single width.

00:17:23.606 --> 00:17:28.440
So let's do a little algebra
and I'll subtract and add C,

00:17:28.441 --> 00:17:30.040
that small constant in

00:17:30.041 --> 00:17:33.100
the appropriate place
and then distribute.

00:17:33.101 --> 00:17:34.845
And now something
nice has happened,

00:17:34.846 --> 00:17:36.670
we have this term here
that's multiplying

00:17:36.671 --> 00:17:39.040
by the modular so,
goodbye to that thing.

00:17:39.041 --> 00:17:40.990
And now there's this kind of

00:17:40.991 --> 00:17:42.700
clever thing that's
happened where we've done

00:17:42.701 --> 00:17:45.190
a modular reduction where
all we need to do is

00:17:45.191 --> 00:17:48.085
multiply by a small
constant and add.

00:17:48.086 --> 00:17:49.600
And now this sum is

00:17:49.601 --> 00:17:52.705
probably though we don't
it's not true in all cases,

00:17:52.706 --> 00:17:54.168
smaller than the modulus.

00:17:54.169 --> 00:17:55.480
And we've done
the mod without using

00:17:55.481 --> 00:17:58.725
any hardware mod instructions
or anything else slow.

00:17:58.726 --> 00:18:02.703
We just need addition
and multiplication.

00:18:02.704 --> 00:18:03.940
So sometimes we even managed to

00:18:03.941 --> 00:18:05.290
do it without
multiplication since

00:18:05.291 --> 00:18:06.550
we know in C we
might implement that

00:18:06.551 --> 00:18:08.675
as several additions
if it's faster.

00:18:08.676 --> 00:18:10.980
So, it's really crucial
that we can do this

00:18:10.981 --> 00:18:14.130
in practice to get
good performance.

00:18:14.131 --> 00:18:16.080
So one thing we will
do in our library,

00:18:16.081 --> 00:18:17.850
is generalize
this trick and make

00:18:17.851 --> 00:18:21.455
our compiler automatically
use it where appropriate.

00:18:21.456 --> 00:18:24.760
So that was at

00:18:24.761 --> 00:18:27.400
the level of the whole result
of multiplication

00:18:27.401 --> 00:18:32.430
assuming we have really big
registers which we don't.

00:18:32.431 --> 00:18:34.820
So, let's think about
this prime number

00:18:34.821 --> 00:18:38.711
and say we're going to
again do a multiplication,

00:18:38.712 --> 00:18:40.360
the output of
multiplication wouldn't

00:18:40.361 --> 00:18:44.460
actually be 510 bits wide.

00:18:44.461 --> 00:18:46.850
And so we could represent it on

00:18:46.851 --> 00:18:49.800
a 64 bit machine with
eight different registers.

00:18:49.801 --> 00:18:53.210
And now let's try to
use that trick once

00:18:53.211 --> 00:18:54.740
we've peeked under
the covers and

00:18:54.741 --> 00:18:56.785
seen how we break
numbers into registers.

00:18:56.786 --> 00:19:02.000
So I've written the formula

00:19:02.001 --> 00:19:03.170
for turning one
of these lists of

00:19:03.171 --> 00:19:04.895
registers back
into a big number.

00:19:04.896 --> 00:19:06.680
And I've written it
suggestively splitting

00:19:06.681 --> 00:19:09.200
the registers into
two halves and

00:19:09.201 --> 00:19:12.290
multiplying by two to the 256 to

00:19:12.291 --> 00:19:14.930
capture shifting
the higher order half

00:19:14.931 --> 00:19:17.700
into the appropriate
position of the answer.

00:19:17.701 --> 00:19:20.750
Unfortunately, that
says two to the

00:19:20.751 --> 00:19:23.540
256 which is not two to 255,

00:19:23.541 --> 00:19:26.060
so the modular reduction
trick that I just

00:19:26.061 --> 00:19:28.490
showed doesn't
apply and that means

00:19:28.491 --> 00:19:31.070
somewhat counterintuitively
this representation

00:19:31.071 --> 00:19:32.450
of big numbers is much much

00:19:32.451 --> 00:19:36.428
slower than the common ones
on commodity processors,

00:19:36.429 --> 00:19:39.375
so this is not a good choice.

00:19:39.376 --> 00:19:41.900
The representation that's
actually used in practice is,

00:19:41.901 --> 00:19:45.110
to use 10 different registers

00:19:45.111 --> 00:19:46.865
that only take up

00:19:46.866 --> 00:19:48.110
51 bits even though we're

00:19:48.111 --> 00:19:50.135
storing them in
64-bit registers.

00:19:50.136 --> 00:19:52.790
So we're kind of
wasting bits but now

00:19:52.791 --> 00:19:55.360
we can use this modular
reduction trick,

00:19:55.361 --> 00:19:57.200
by the way at
various points we'll allow

00:19:57.201 --> 00:19:59.900
the registers to grow
larger than 51 bits and we'll

00:19:59.901 --> 00:20:01.490
carefully bound
how much they grow

00:20:01.491 --> 00:20:03.260
with each operation
that eventually

00:20:03.261 --> 00:20:05.150
we'll carry and reduce

00:20:05.151 --> 00:20:08.165
down to get back to
the original representation.

00:20:08.166 --> 00:20:11.795
So, the most widely used
64-bit representations

00:20:11.796 --> 00:20:14.325
use this trick.

00:20:14.326 --> 00:20:16.493
And then the 32 bit,

00:20:16.494 --> 00:20:19.605
the way it's usually put is,

00:20:19.606 --> 00:20:23.460
we'll take 20 registers,

00:20:23.461 --> 00:20:26.195
each of size 25.5 bits,

00:20:26.196 --> 00:20:30.410
and get a representation
like this one and of

00:20:30.411 --> 00:20:33.200
course a little expensive to

00:20:33.201 --> 00:20:36.350
get fractional bit registers
on commodity processors.

00:20:36.351 --> 00:20:37.550
So what that actually means is

00:20:37.551 --> 00:20:41.360
let's take a floor
or ceiling operation

00:20:41.361 --> 00:20:43.610
in the appropriate places
to get these bit widths

00:20:43.611 --> 00:20:47.150
to be whole numbers.

00:20:47.151 --> 00:20:49.018
So, now we get,

00:20:49.019 --> 00:20:50.690
not only do we
have some choice in

00:20:50.691 --> 00:20:53.189
how many registers to
split the number into,

00:20:53.190 --> 00:20:54.620
not only do we choose
how many of the bits

00:20:54.621 --> 00:20:56.390
and the registers
will we actually use,

00:20:56.391 --> 00:20:58.640
we might actually make
non-uniform choices

00:20:58.641 --> 00:21:01.755
across the different registers
that we're using.

00:21:01.756 --> 00:21:04.620
So this is the standard
32 bit implementation.

00:21:04.621 --> 00:21:06.525
So at a high level
what we're doing,

00:21:06.526 --> 00:21:09.540
is we're building
a compiler or a framework,

00:21:09.541 --> 00:21:12.300
few different words
apply that is

00:21:12.301 --> 00:21:14.220
parameterized on
choices like this,

00:21:14.221 --> 00:21:16.380
and given that
any choice guarantees to

00:21:16.381 --> 00:21:20.130
compile correct code
with a Calc theorem that

00:21:20.131 --> 00:21:22.560
the arithmetic operations
are actually computing

00:21:22.561 --> 00:21:26.888
the original whiteboard
level number theory stuff.

00:21:26.889 --> 00:21:29.035
So, our library takes in

00:21:29.036 --> 00:21:30.390
your choice of representation

00:21:30.391 --> 00:21:31.965
in that space of possibilities,

00:21:31.966 --> 00:21:33.300
and spits out fast

00:21:33.301 --> 00:21:35.190
cosi assembly code at
the moment we actually

00:21:35.191 --> 00:21:37.047
assume infinitely
many temporaries

00:21:37.048 --> 00:21:40.155
everything else is
very assembly code looking.

00:21:40.156 --> 00:21:42.545
And you get approved
to the library,

00:21:42.546 --> 00:21:43.950
parts of it approved
once and for all,

00:21:43.951 --> 00:21:46.780
parts of it are
approved generating.

00:21:46.781 --> 00:21:49.080
So, in a little more detail
what's going on

00:21:49.081 --> 00:21:51.525
inside that green oval,

00:21:51.526 --> 00:21:54.705
we have a library of

00:21:54.706 --> 00:21:56.730
Haskell like
functional programs and

00:21:56.731 --> 00:21:59.485
Calc that implement
all the key arithmetic.

00:21:59.486 --> 00:22:02.880
And then we use partial
evaluation after you choose

00:22:02.881 --> 00:22:04.800
a particular digit
representation

00:22:04.801 --> 00:22:06.840
to specialize it to
your particular choice.

00:22:06.841 --> 00:22:09.210
Now we get flatter functional
programs that look even

00:22:09.211 --> 00:22:11.580
more like assembly code
and then we do

00:22:11.581 --> 00:22:12.990
bounds inference
and other compiler

00:22:12.991 --> 00:22:14.790
optimizations to generate

00:22:14.791 --> 00:22:19.780
the actual low level code
that comes out in the end.

00:22:19.781 --> 00:22:21.210
So here's an example of one of

00:22:21.211 --> 00:22:23.985
the kinds of algorithms
we need to deal with.

00:22:23.986 --> 00:22:26.551
Let's look at
multiplication modulus

00:22:26.552 --> 00:22:27.960
two to the 127 minus 1,

00:22:27.961 --> 00:22:30.660
a simpler version of the prime
I was showing before.

00:22:30.661 --> 00:22:31.770
So now, we might split each of

00:22:31.771 --> 00:22:33.360
the numbers into
three different digits,

00:22:33.361 --> 00:22:37.356
and they translate back
into big numbers like so.

00:22:37.357 --> 00:22:40.250
So, one way to do

00:22:40.251 --> 00:22:41.825
this multiplication
is just compute

00:22:41.826 --> 00:22:43.917
all the cross terms
between S and T,

00:22:43.918 --> 00:22:45.800
we get a bunch of
terms like this,

00:22:45.801 --> 00:22:48.575
and I have lined them up
suggestively here because

00:22:48.576 --> 00:22:49.850
down the columns we see

00:22:49.851 --> 00:22:52.220
the powers of two are
very close to each other.

00:22:52.221 --> 00:22:54.725
So, we can just add
down the columns

00:22:54.726 --> 00:22:58.425
and produce an output like so,

00:22:58.426 --> 00:23:01.160
and you can see in
some places the powers of

00:23:01.161 --> 00:23:03.530
two were a little
off from each other.

00:23:03.531 --> 00:23:06.915
So, we do extra multiplication
to compensate there.

00:23:06.916 --> 00:23:10.250
And now, we're almost

00:23:10.251 --> 00:23:12.965
done except we have
a double wide result here,

00:23:12.966 --> 00:23:15.440
meaning we have five digits,

00:23:15.441 --> 00:23:17.140
where we started out with
only three in the numbers.

00:23:17.141 --> 00:23:20.270
We want to reduce
this down to being

00:23:20.271 --> 00:23:24.125
just three digits using
a process called carrying.

00:23:24.126 --> 00:23:28.790
And the way that works is we
notice the last two digits,

00:23:28.791 --> 00:23:31.090
we can write the formula

00:23:31.091 --> 00:23:33.445
to convert back into
one number like this,

00:23:33.446 --> 00:23:35.035
with the last two digits are

00:23:35.036 --> 00:23:38.095
shifted by multiplying
by 2 to the 127,

00:23:38.096 --> 00:23:41.230
and then because 2 to the 127 is

00:23:41.231 --> 00:23:44.330
congruent to 1 in the prime
field we're working in,

00:23:44.331 --> 00:23:49.120
we can move those digits to
appropriate other positions.

00:23:49.121 --> 00:23:51.820
For instance, U3 move

00:23:51.821 --> 00:23:53.220
from all the way
over there to here,

00:23:53.221 --> 00:23:55.740
and U4 move to the second digit.

00:23:55.741 --> 00:23:59.500
Now, we are back in the
appropriate three digit format.

00:23:59.501 --> 00:24:02.470
So, we write a functional
program that does this

00:24:02.471 --> 00:24:05.924
generically for any choice of
base-system representation.

00:24:05.925 --> 00:24:07.480
We proved that functional
program correct,

00:24:07.481 --> 00:24:08.365
once and for all.

00:24:08.366 --> 00:24:09.780
So, we can just specialize it

00:24:09.781 --> 00:24:11.410
to new representation choices,

00:24:11.411 --> 00:24:16.246
and automatically know that
you have the correct code.

00:24:16.247 --> 00:24:20.075
But it's not as easy as

00:24:20.076 --> 00:24:21.530
just saying that there are

00:24:21.531 --> 00:24:23.420
some representation choices that

00:24:23.421 --> 00:24:24.895
have a big effect on how,

00:24:24.896 --> 00:24:26.710
or easy the prove
started, right.

00:24:26.711 --> 00:24:28.670
So, you might think we
would want to formalize

00:24:28.671 --> 00:24:30.140
based systems or how we

00:24:30.141 --> 00:24:31.890
divide numbers into
digits like this.

00:24:31.891 --> 00:24:33.860
Let's have a weight function W

00:24:33.861 --> 00:24:37.460
that for each position in
the number representation,

00:24:37.461 --> 00:24:39.080
tells us what we multiply

00:24:39.081 --> 00:24:42.150
that position by to reconstruct
the original number.

00:24:42.151 --> 00:24:44.190
So, and we'll say
the first position

00:24:44.191 --> 00:24:45.770
zero needs to have weight one.

00:24:45.771 --> 00:24:48.223
That's the least
significant digit.

00:24:48.224 --> 00:24:51.130
And all the other weights
need to be non-zero.

00:24:51.131 --> 00:24:52.670
So then when we have
a list like this,

00:24:52.671 --> 00:24:54.500
we convert it back
into a number by

00:24:54.501 --> 00:24:56.955
multiplying each position
by the associated weight,

00:24:56.956 --> 00:25:00.335
just adding all those together.

00:25:00.336 --> 00:25:03.640
So, this turns out to

00:25:03.641 --> 00:25:07.030
be a rather hard to reason
about doing the proofs.

00:25:07.031 --> 00:25:09.640
Whenever you are doing

00:25:09.641 --> 00:25:11.870
a multiplication like
on this previous slide,

00:25:11.871 --> 00:25:13.930
you get all these cross terms,

00:25:13.931 --> 00:25:15.850
and you have to ask yourself,

00:25:15.851 --> 00:25:20.200
is the weight of this digit
already in my base system,

00:25:20.201 --> 00:25:22.480
or do I need to do
some sort of conversion to

00:25:22.481 --> 00:25:24.845
get it to match what
my base system supports?

00:25:24.846 --> 00:25:28.495
So, for instance, this 2 to
the 85 versus 2 to the 86.

00:25:28.496 --> 00:25:30.250
One of those is
going to be not in

00:25:30.251 --> 00:25:32.440
the original
base-system and we have

00:25:32.441 --> 00:25:33.850
to do some special work to

00:25:33.851 --> 00:25:37.045
convert between
weights of digits.

00:25:37.046 --> 00:25:38.650
And other little things

00:25:38.651 --> 00:25:39.880
like that we're
just building up in

00:25:39.881 --> 00:25:41.080
complexity when we were writing

00:25:41.081 --> 00:25:42.460
the code and doing the proofs.

00:25:42.461 --> 00:25:43.690
So, instead, we decided on

00:25:43.691 --> 00:25:46.225
a different representation
called the Associational,

00:25:46.226 --> 00:25:49.510
where we represent numbers
as lists of pairs of

00:25:49.511 --> 00:25:50.890
digits and this kind of

00:25:50.891 --> 00:25:53.285
static weights that are
associated with them.

00:25:53.286 --> 00:25:56.320
So, for instance,
if we represent

00:25:56.321 --> 00:25:59.680
the number 234 with
the usual decimal system,

00:25:59.681 --> 00:26:03.190
then it gets translated to
that list over there that

00:26:03.191 --> 00:26:07.143
just has the digits.

00:26:07.144 --> 00:26:08.635
I think I meant to put,

00:26:08.636 --> 00:26:10.963
something is a
little wrong here.

00:26:10.964 --> 00:26:13.140
This looks like a two

00:26:13.141 --> 00:26:14.820
in the last position
instead of a four.

00:26:14.821 --> 00:26:16.440
Anyway, we take the digits,

00:26:16.441 --> 00:26:18.060
and we pair them
with their weights

00:26:18.061 --> 00:26:19.275
which are powers of 10

00:26:19.276 --> 00:26:27.840
and get that list.

00:26:27.841 --> 00:26:28.080
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] If it was 204,
we need to have 10, 0 or not?

00:26:28.081 --> 00:26:30.570
&gt;&gt; The question is if one
of the digits is zero,

00:26:30.571 --> 00:26:33.050
do we include an explicit entry
for that in the list.

00:26:33.051 --> 00:26:34.830
And I think we do
because usually,

00:26:34.831 --> 00:26:36.150
the actual digit values are

00:26:36.151 --> 00:26:37.560
variables rather than constant.

00:26:37.561 --> 00:26:41.632
So, we don't know
what's zero in advance.

00:26:41.633 --> 00:26:43.620
All right.

00:26:43.621 --> 00:26:45.640
But we were going to

00:26:45.641 --> 00:26:48.370
generalize this and allow
a lot of flexibility in

00:26:48.371 --> 00:26:50.875
how we split numbers up

00:26:50.876 --> 00:26:52.690
and we're going to
allow lists like

00:26:52.691 --> 00:26:54.675
this which may even
have duplicate weights.

00:26:54.676 --> 00:26:56.170
So, for instance, we
could take this list and

00:26:56.171 --> 00:26:58.365
add something else
multiplied by 100.

00:26:58.366 --> 00:27:00.670
And we're not going to fix
the weights in advance,

00:27:00.671 --> 00:27:03.375
because an intermediate stages
during algorithms,

00:27:03.376 --> 00:27:06.170
all sorts of strange temporary
weights might pop up,

00:27:06.171 --> 00:27:10.510
and we want to be
prepared for any of those.

00:27:10.511 --> 00:27:12.310
So, given a list like this,

00:27:12.311 --> 00:27:14.228
it's pretty easy to write
a Coq functional program

00:27:14.229 --> 00:27:16.645
that translates the list
back into one big number,

00:27:16.646 --> 00:27:19.145
which we use to state
the correctness properties.

00:27:19.146 --> 00:27:20.670
The actual running code
never does this.

00:27:20.671 --> 00:27:21.820
We don't have registers big

00:27:21.821 --> 00:27:23.330
enough to store these numbers,

00:27:23.331 --> 00:27:25.503
but it's a very simple map
followed by a fold-right,

00:27:25.504 --> 00:27:27.520
kind of thing which I'm
sure any of you could

00:27:27.521 --> 00:27:29.703
have written in your sleep.

00:27:29.704 --> 00:27:31.900
But the consequences
of this are really

00:27:31.901 --> 00:27:34.475
great for simplicity
of the proofs.

00:27:34.476 --> 00:27:38.360
So, for instance, we want
to implement addition.

00:27:38.361 --> 00:27:40.865
Does anyone have
an implementation of

00:27:40.866 --> 00:27:43.125
addition in mind for
this representation?

00:27:43.126 --> 00:27:45.655
It becomes really trivial now.

00:27:45.656 --> 00:27:48.230
Yeah, it's just
list concatenation.

00:27:48.231 --> 00:27:52.510
And so, we can just prove
that the evaluation of

00:27:52.511 --> 00:27:54.120
a concatenated list
is the sum of

00:27:54.121 --> 00:27:58.115
the two values of
the original lists.

00:27:58.116 --> 00:28:00.450
And this is a big
simplification over what

00:28:00.451 --> 00:28:05.295
we need to do with
the static base system approach.

00:28:05.296 --> 00:28:07.990
And it has a pretty
short Coq of two.

00:28:07.991 --> 00:28:09.960
So, to do multiplication,

00:28:09.961 --> 00:28:11.880
we just want to compute
all the cross terms

00:28:11.881 --> 00:28:13.335
between the two lists,

00:28:13.336 --> 00:28:16.385
and that's pretty
straightforward.

00:28:16.386 --> 00:28:20.205
And so, we can define
multiplication like this,

00:28:20.206 --> 00:28:22.650
take a map and

00:28:22.651 --> 00:28:24.545
a flat map and put them
together in the right way,

00:28:24.546 --> 00:28:26.160
and you can prove a theorem
like this one that

00:28:26.161 --> 00:28:28.620
multiplication of this kind
really corresponds to

00:28:28.621 --> 00:28:30.990
multiplication of the usual kind

00:28:30.991 --> 00:28:33.975
with pushing evaluation
through the operation.

00:28:33.976 --> 00:28:36.190
And that has a pretty
short proof as well.

00:28:36.191 --> 00:28:39.090
And really, all the other
arithmetic operations

00:28:39.091 --> 00:28:42.405
we need go similarly
smoothly to this.

00:28:42.406 --> 00:28:49.840
But modular reduction is
a little more complicated,

00:28:49.841 --> 00:28:51.615
not too much more.

00:28:51.616 --> 00:28:55.030
So, remember I talked
about this trick

00:28:55.031 --> 00:28:59.680
where we reduced
the modular reduction operation

00:28:59.681 --> 00:29:01.570
to just multiplying and adding.

00:29:01.571 --> 00:29:04.525
And we can generalize that to

00:29:04.526 --> 00:29:07.180
the full space of base systems,

00:29:07.181 --> 00:29:09.010
where we need
an operation called split

00:29:09.011 --> 00:29:11.395
that takes one of these lists,

00:29:11.396 --> 00:29:12.970
and breaks it into the terms

00:29:12.971 --> 00:29:15.160
whose weights are above

00:29:15.161 --> 00:29:17.210
a threshold and
below a threshold.

00:29:17.211 --> 00:29:19.090
And that turns out to
be easier to write to,

00:29:19.091 --> 00:29:20.290
we just use a classic list

00:29:20.291 --> 00:29:22.255
partition operation that checks,

00:29:22.256 --> 00:29:23.590
it looks at the weights,

00:29:23.591 --> 00:29:27.205
and sees which ones a zero
the amount we're splitting by,

00:29:27.206 --> 00:29:31.570
and then keeps the high part
of that partition the same,

00:29:31.571 --> 00:29:33.340
and then reduces
the low part where

00:29:33.341 --> 00:29:36.460
everything is divisible
by this weight.

00:29:36.461 --> 00:29:38.050
Actually does the division,

00:29:38.051 --> 00:29:40.540
and then we're good there,

00:29:40.541 --> 00:29:42.360
and then the reduced
operation that we need,

00:29:42.361 --> 00:29:43.570
performs the split, and then

00:29:43.571 --> 00:29:45.160
does the multiplying and adds

00:29:45.161 --> 00:29:46.690
because remember we implement

00:29:46.691 --> 00:29:49.245
add with just
concatenation of lists.

00:29:49.246 --> 00:29:51.140
So, this too is quite
straightforward.

00:29:51.141 --> 00:29:53.305
But there has to
be a catch, right?

00:29:53.306 --> 00:29:55.115
Because we're working with
this crazy representation,

00:29:55.116 --> 00:29:56.800
and in the end we
want the actual

00:29:56.801 --> 00:29:59.845
running assembly level code

00:29:59.846 --> 00:30:02.495
to be using a fixed
digit representation.

00:30:02.496 --> 00:30:05.680
So, the way that works is
we write code like this one,

00:30:05.681 --> 00:30:07.630
which takes inputs
that are actually

00:30:07.631 --> 00:30:09.600
using dependent types
to enforce,

00:30:09.601 --> 00:30:13.820
say this tuple Z n-type is
a n-tuple of elements of Z.

00:30:13.821 --> 00:30:17.200
And we first, take
the regular representation,

00:30:17.201 --> 00:30:20.425
convert each of the inputs
into the associational form,

00:30:20.426 --> 00:30:23.710
then do the computation
in a nice representation,

00:30:23.711 --> 00:30:25.120
and then go back out from

00:30:25.121 --> 00:30:27.955
associational to
the normal representation.

00:30:27.956 --> 00:30:30.220
You might be worried
looking at this code

00:30:30.221 --> 00:30:31.365
that we're going to have

00:30:31.366 --> 00:30:33.490
run-time overhead of constantly

00:30:33.491 --> 00:30:35.530
converting back and
forth between formats.

00:30:35.531 --> 00:30:37.825
The idea is for a given function

00:30:37.826 --> 00:30:39.430
that's present in
the code in the end,

00:30:39.431 --> 00:30:41.620
we only convert once

00:30:41.621 --> 00:30:44.000
at the beginning of
the code and once the end,

00:30:44.001 --> 00:30:45.160
and we're going to use

00:30:45.161 --> 00:30:47.770
partial evaluation to
remove all the overhead

00:30:47.771 --> 00:30:49.690
of this conversion such that

00:30:49.691 --> 00:30:52.015
our proofs are a lot
easier by the fact that,

00:30:52.016 --> 00:30:53.595
the reasoning in
the middle here is

00:30:53.596 --> 00:30:56.080
straightforward and we will also

00:30:56.081 --> 00:30:57.870
need to prove that
these two associational and

00:30:57.871 --> 00:31:00.340
prime associational
operations are correct.

00:31:00.341 --> 00:31:01.645
They have to do a little bit of

00:31:01.646 --> 00:31:05.200
arithmetic to
choose the right way

00:31:05.201 --> 00:31:07.270
to convert when you
have an input term

00:31:07.271 --> 00:31:08.830
whose weights don't quite

00:31:08.831 --> 00:31:10.110
match the ones
you're looking for.

00:31:10.111 --> 00:31:12.040
But we do that once,
we prove that once,

00:31:12.041 --> 00:31:14.755
and then the other code
and its proofs

00:31:14.756 --> 00:31:15.970
follow this form and

00:31:15.971 --> 00:31:18.115
are pretty
straightforward to verify.

00:31:18.116 --> 00:31:20.440
So, in other words, let's say,

00:31:20.441 --> 00:31:21.820
we have the example
of multiply or

00:31:21.821 --> 00:31:23.835
some other arithmetic function,

00:31:23.836 --> 00:31:26.785
it's parameterized
over the bitwidths

00:31:26.786 --> 00:31:28.030
of the digits that come

00:31:28.031 --> 00:31:30.010
in over the actual values of

00:31:30.011 --> 00:31:32.305
the digits and then it
produces the multiplication.

00:31:32.306 --> 00:31:33.605
We think of the bitwidths as

00:31:33.606 --> 00:31:35.290
a compile-time fixed parameter,

00:31:35.291 --> 00:31:39.580
and the actual digits as
a run-time varying parameter.

00:31:39.581 --> 00:31:41.985
So, we do specialization,

00:31:41.986 --> 00:31:44.815
just think of this as
a curried function,

00:31:44.816 --> 00:31:46.920
so that just passing it
one of the arguments,

00:31:46.921 --> 00:31:49.330
and not the others produces
a Coq term that stands for

00:31:49.331 --> 00:31:52.620
the representation
specialized multiplication.

00:31:52.621 --> 00:31:55.400
And then, we just reduced
that with partial evaluation

00:31:55.401 --> 00:31:58.915
until it gets rid of all the
overhead of the generality.

00:31:58.916 --> 00:32:00.670
And Coq is a really
nice platform

00:32:00.671 --> 00:32:01.840
for doing this because the

00:32:01.841 --> 00:32:03.460
specialized stages
just partially

00:32:03.461 --> 00:32:04.790
applying a curried function.

00:32:04.791 --> 00:32:06.370
The reduced stage
is just calling

00:32:06.371 --> 00:32:08.935
standard term-reduction
features that

00:32:08.936 --> 00:32:11.245
use lambda calculus rules
to reduce until,

00:32:11.246 --> 00:32:14.775
suddenly the code looks
like assembly, more or less.

00:32:14.776 --> 00:32:18.965
So, these steps are not
only are they easy in Coq,

00:32:18.966 --> 00:32:20.360
but for those in the know about

00:32:20.361 --> 00:32:21.985
type theory based proof systems,

00:32:21.986 --> 00:32:24.845
they basically produce
proof terms that

00:32:24.846 --> 00:32:30.783
are essentially just
restating the problem.

00:32:30.784 --> 00:32:33.205
You don't need to do any
explicit proof manipulations.

00:32:33.206 --> 00:32:35.190
These steps are
built into the logic

00:32:35.191 --> 00:32:40.070
as automatically applied for
you whenever you need them.

00:32:40.400 --> 00:32:43.780
So, here's an example

00:32:43.781 --> 00:32:46.780
of the end product
of that approach.

00:32:46.781 --> 00:32:48.910
So I've defined
a particular base system.

00:32:48.911 --> 00:32:52.085
This is the example I
gave of the popular prime

00:32:52.086 --> 00:32:55.735
where you need to split it
into 20 different digits,

00:32:55.736 --> 00:33:00.670
each based on alternating

00:33:00.671 --> 00:33:02.540
between two
different bit widths,

00:33:02.541 --> 00:33:04.355
and this function at
the top specifies that.

00:33:04.356 --> 00:33:06.670
So a static Coq
proof call saying,

00:33:06.671 --> 00:33:08.980
"Please find me
an inhabitant of this set,

00:33:08.981 --> 00:33:12.715
given two tuples of width 10."

00:33:12.716 --> 00:33:15.100
So apparently we only have 10.

00:33:15.101 --> 00:33:16.600
I guess this might be
a simplified version

00:33:16.601 --> 00:33:18.390
of that example.

00:33:18.391 --> 00:33:23.060
Give me F, G which is another
tuple of width 10, where,

00:33:23.061 --> 00:33:27.460
when I evaluate it in
this choice of widths,

00:33:27.461 --> 00:33:30.400
we get out the same thing
as if I evaluated

00:33:30.401 --> 00:33:31.840
the two inputs
and multiply those

00:33:31.841 --> 00:33:34.335
together in our
particular modules.

00:33:34.336 --> 00:33:35.880
And then, drum roll please,

00:33:35.881 --> 00:33:39.910
I run the framework
and out pops this code.

00:33:39.911 --> 00:33:42.520
Probably should have
introduced the bug on

00:33:42.521 --> 00:33:44.385
purpose and asked anybody
if they could catch it.

00:33:44.386 --> 00:33:49.120
But this is pretty nice
flat-looking code that can

00:33:49.121 --> 00:33:51.920
be turned into
C-like code by just

00:33:51.921 --> 00:33:55.175
introducing names for
the intermediate computations.

00:33:55.176 --> 00:33:58.550
And all these numbers
like 38 and 19

00:33:58.551 --> 00:34:02.115
popped into existence
at the right spots,

00:34:02.116 --> 00:34:06.920
and no new proofs needed to
be written for this to work,

00:34:06.921 --> 00:34:08.560
but we do get
a guarantee that this is

00:34:08.561 --> 00:34:10.855
a correct realization
of all that.

00:34:10.856 --> 00:34:12.160
We have some Coq tactics that

00:34:12.161 --> 00:34:13.610
given the statement
of a proof call will

00:34:13.611 --> 00:34:17.790
automatically do what's
needed to get to this point.

00:34:17.791 --> 00:34:21.505
&gt;&gt; I'm a little confused
about something. If you

00:34:21.506 --> 00:34:24.274
take a handwritten
implementation

00:34:24.275 --> 00:34:30.274
to build this kind of operation
on this representation,

00:34:30.275 --> 00:34:33.000
every operation is guaranteed.

00:34:33.001 --> 00:34:35.320
It carefully written to stay in

00:34:35.321 --> 00:34:40.580
this particular representation
of this 25 and half bit.

00:34:40.581 --> 00:34:44.050
It's not obvious to
me that if you convert

00:34:44.051 --> 00:34:45.640
to associational form
and then go

00:34:45.641 --> 00:34:47.530
back that you will also
always stay in that form.

00:34:47.531 --> 00:34:49.225
&gt;&gt; Got It.

00:34:49.226 --> 00:34:51.580
So, the question is given

00:34:51.581 --> 00:34:54.220
this framework
we've set up that is

00:34:54.221 --> 00:34:57.390
so flexible about the width

00:34:57.391 --> 00:34:59.350
of digits at different points,

00:34:59.351 --> 00:35:01.720
how can we be sure that
the code we generate will

00:35:01.721 --> 00:35:04.905
actually stay committed
to one representation?

00:35:04.906 --> 00:35:07.325
The answer is the general
framework does not ensure that,

00:35:07.326 --> 00:35:09.910
but we heuristically choose
the right instantiations of

00:35:09.911 --> 00:35:13.720
its parameters so that
in practice this happens.

00:35:13.721 --> 00:35:15.950
And so it's at least
true if you get

00:35:15.951 --> 00:35:18.253
that heuristic choice wrong,

00:35:18.254 --> 00:35:20.315
you don't get functionally
incorrect code out,

00:35:20.316 --> 00:35:22.190
but you might get
really slow code.

00:35:22.191 --> 00:35:25.265
So, you can even do

00:35:25.266 --> 00:35:27.230
genetic search
through a space of

00:35:27.231 --> 00:35:29.360
parameters and
benchmark everything,

00:35:29.361 --> 00:35:31.050
and keep the fast ones so

00:35:31.051 --> 00:35:32.870
that you can get
good performance

00:35:32.871 --> 00:35:34.665
without baking in to

00:35:34.666 --> 00:35:36.800
the formal side what
good performance means.

00:35:36.801 --> 00:35:40.370
&gt;&gt; So at one point,

00:35:40.371 --> 00:35:45.370
you have each one of
these small variables

00:35:45.371 --> 00:35:51.040
like F2, F9, and so
on that are bounded.

00:35:51.041 --> 00:35:51.715
&gt;&gt; Oh that's
two slides from now.

00:35:51.716 --> 00:35:53.065
That's when I know the bound,

00:35:53.066 --> 00:35:56.305
that none of these overflow,
which is important.

00:35:56.306 --> 00:35:59.920
&gt;&gt; You talked about
the formal performance.

00:35:59.921 --> 00:36:01.460
I gather that that's not at

00:36:01.461 --> 00:36:03.695
all what you're
interested in right now.

00:36:03.696 --> 00:36:06.030
Can you imagine
a scenario in which

00:36:06.031 --> 00:36:08.305
you'd be able to prove
some bound nonperformance

00:36:08.306 --> 00:36:10.645
at least given us

00:36:10.646 --> 00:36:14.215
a limited bag of tricks or
maybe not limiting your bag?

00:36:14.216 --> 00:36:17.865
&gt;&gt; So, could we someday
prove performance results?

00:36:17.866 --> 00:36:20.515
It seems very believable

00:36:20.516 --> 00:36:22.715
given that we're basically
doing straight line code here.

00:36:22.716 --> 00:36:23.950
How hard can it be?

00:36:23.951 --> 00:36:26.920
Optimality, can we prove that

00:36:26.921 --> 00:36:28.450
there is no better
implementation of

00:36:28.451 --> 00:36:30.370
this algorithm on
this processor?

00:36:30.371 --> 00:36:32.275
I have thought about
doing that because

00:36:32.276 --> 00:36:34.570
we also work on
processor verification.

00:36:34.571 --> 00:36:36.040
So, we can look at
the Verilog code

00:36:36.041 --> 00:36:37.685
for the processor and
try to prove that.

00:36:37.686 --> 00:36:39.175
But I have no concrete ideas

00:36:39.176 --> 00:36:41.225
on what the proof
strategy would be.

00:36:41.226 --> 00:36:43.640
Sounds like fun though.

00:36:43.641 --> 00:36:44.640
&gt;&gt; This is not necessary

00:36:44.641 --> 00:36:48.330
a very optimal algorithm
carrot super and stuff,

00:36:48.331 --> 00:36:51.255
&gt;&gt; Right. We do have carrot
super in our library,

00:36:51.256 --> 00:36:54.750
but it's not being used
here I think, yeah.

00:36:54.751 --> 00:37:00.760
I think char super

00:37:00.761 --> 00:37:04.005
turned out to be not so bad
in this funky representation,

00:37:04.006 --> 00:37:05.850
but I don't remember
the details.

00:37:05.851 --> 00:37:11.240
&gt;&gt; Another question, when
you pick these heuristics,

00:37:11.241 --> 00:37:17.420
do you expect to get out

00:37:17.421 --> 00:37:20.635
the code that corresponds

00:37:20.636 --> 00:37:22.372
to a handwritten implementation

00:37:22.373 --> 00:37:24.040
that you're trying to match?

00:37:24.041 --> 00:37:25.630
&gt;&gt; Pretty much,
yeah. The question

00:37:25.631 --> 00:37:26.800
is will we expect to get

00:37:26.801 --> 00:37:29.846
the same code that the experts
are writing by hand.

00:37:29.847 --> 00:37:33.260
What tends to happen is it's
not literally the same code,

00:37:33.261 --> 00:37:34.670
but it's a one line proven

00:37:34.671 --> 00:37:36.530
Coq to establish
it's the same code.

00:37:36.531 --> 00:37:38.045
The instructions are
in different orders,

00:37:38.046 --> 00:37:40.130
which do have consequences for

00:37:40.131 --> 00:37:42.595
registry allocation
and that sort of stuff.

00:37:42.596 --> 00:37:45.350
When it's still an open problem,

00:37:45.351 --> 00:37:46.550
how to automatically make

00:37:46.551 --> 00:37:50.755
those choices in
a very efficient way.

00:37:50.756 --> 00:37:53.950
There are no deep
differences in our code

00:37:53.951 --> 00:37:56.185
versus the handwritten code.

00:37:56.186 --> 00:37:58.038
All right.

00:37:58.039 --> 00:38:00.070
There is one other catch

00:38:00.071 --> 00:38:01.630
that shows up in
partial evaluation,

00:38:01.631 --> 00:38:04.510
which this example is
too simple to need.

00:38:04.511 --> 00:38:08.020
Sometimes we have
common subterms

00:38:08.021 --> 00:38:10.140
within a big term like this.

00:38:10.141 --> 00:38:12.460
Not so many here because
sort of by construction,

00:38:12.461 --> 00:38:15.580
we're always getting
these cross products

00:38:15.581 --> 00:38:17.420
of different terms of the input,

00:38:17.421 --> 00:38:20.185
but sometimes we need to
preserve term structure.

00:38:20.186 --> 00:38:22.435
So, let me give an example,

00:38:22.436 --> 00:38:24.125
here I'm just trying
to motivate the idea.

00:38:24.126 --> 00:38:26.665
Sometimes we want to generate
code with let's in it,

00:38:26.666 --> 00:38:29.230
and have those be
preserved throughout

00:38:29.231 --> 00:38:32.400
the evaluation
down to flat code.

00:38:32.401 --> 00:38:35.800
So, imagine the lets
in this simple example,

00:38:35.801 --> 00:38:37.425
we're binding variables that

00:38:37.426 --> 00:38:39.250
needed to be mentioned
multiple times.

00:38:39.251 --> 00:38:41.530
Though in this case, to
keep it straightforward I'm

00:38:41.531 --> 00:38:44.080
only having each let
be mention once.

00:38:44.081 --> 00:38:48.070
This is a simplified case of
an addition algorithm that

00:38:48.071 --> 00:38:49.705
is just component wise adding

00:38:49.706 --> 00:38:52.350
the digits between two inputs.

00:38:52.351 --> 00:38:54.655
And by the way,

00:38:54.656 --> 00:38:56.530
you might notice
this simple code

00:38:56.531 --> 00:38:58.045
doesn't do carrying
across digits,

00:38:58.046 --> 00:38:59.470
but actually
many real algorithms

00:38:59.471 --> 00:39:00.700
set things up very carefully,

00:39:00.701 --> 00:39:03.135
so that you don't need to
do carrying with every add,

00:39:03.136 --> 00:39:06.250
and only after a few
you have accumulated

00:39:06.251 --> 00:39:08.440
enough extra bits in

00:39:08.441 --> 00:39:10.380
your registers that
you then do some work.

00:39:10.381 --> 00:39:11.465
But imagine we're trying to add

00:39:11.466 --> 00:39:12.700
two lists using this function.

00:39:12.701 --> 00:39:13.840
And now, I want

00:39:13.841 --> 00:39:17.780
to specialize this code to
the case of three digits.

00:39:17.781 --> 00:39:21.140
In each input, there
are some six values

00:39:21.141 --> 00:39:22.585
that are not known
at compile time.

00:39:22.586 --> 00:39:23.795
And I just tell Coq,

00:39:23.796 --> 00:39:26.195
evaluate this functional program
in the usual way.

00:39:26.196 --> 00:39:27.590
Unfortunately, we
get this kind of

00:39:27.591 --> 00:39:29.900
ugly thing which doesn't
look like assembly of

00:39:29.901 --> 00:39:33.865
the lets are nested inside
of the list Coq operations.

00:39:33.866 --> 00:39:35.345
So, the way we
get around that is

00:39:35.346 --> 00:39:37.250
unfortunately, but it
works well in the end.

00:39:37.251 --> 00:39:39.985
We write most of our code in
continuation passing style.

00:39:39.986 --> 00:39:42.635
So here's the CPS version
of add lists,

00:39:42.636 --> 00:39:47.335
where say the recursive call

00:39:47.336 --> 00:39:49.990
is in tail position and we
build a new continuation,

00:39:49.991 --> 00:39:52.345
and standard stuff like that.

00:39:52.346 --> 00:39:56.530
And then, when we try to do
the same kind of reduction on

00:39:56.531 --> 00:40:00.400
the lists of length three
with unknown values,

00:40:00.401 --> 00:40:02.585
then it just reduces
to the right point.

00:40:02.586 --> 00:40:04.055
So, it looks really
straightforward here.

00:40:04.056 --> 00:40:05.381
It's kind of a pain
in our code base.

00:40:05.382 --> 00:40:06.640
We wrote everything CPS

00:40:06.641 --> 00:40:08.590
and direct style
improved equivalence.

00:40:08.591 --> 00:40:10.960
And the actual normalization
only happens in

00:40:10.961 --> 00:40:14.650
CPS to get proper sharing
of comments subterms.

00:40:14.651 --> 00:40:18.130
It took us a long time to
figure this out by the way.

00:40:18.131 --> 00:40:23.352
&gt;&gt; I'm sorry, why
does X plus Y not

00:40:23.353 --> 00:40:23.490
get like in the continuation?

00:40:23.491 --> 00:40:27.770
&gt;&gt; All right, because we've

00:40:27.771 --> 00:40:32.145
told Coq not to do the let
in lining rule of reduction,

00:40:32.146 --> 00:40:36.460
but to do all the others or
an appropriate set of others.

00:40:36.461 --> 00:40:37.460
&gt;&gt; But could've done
that in the first one.

00:40:37.461 --> 00:40:41.205
How does the
continuation help you?

00:40:41.206 --> 00:40:43.630
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's kind of magic.

00:40:43.631 --> 00:40:47.455
I don't have a great
description of why.

00:40:47.456 --> 00:40:49.540
I think this trick is
sort of well-known in

00:40:49.541 --> 00:40:51.730
the partial
evaluation world, but-

00:40:51.731 --> 00:40:53.110
&gt;&gt; You can see

00:40:53.111 --> 00:40:57.110
the length before you go
to the continuation section

00:40:57.111 --> 00:41:00.110
in the second version,
whereas in the first version,

00:41:00.111 --> 00:41:03.110
you do the Coq and then you
do address the [inaudible].

00:41:03.111 --> 00:41:06.400
&gt;&gt; By the way,

00:41:06.401 --> 00:41:08.283
I forgot to repeat
the question which was

00:41:08.284 --> 00:41:10.540
basically why does this

00:41:10.541 --> 00:41:12.970
help? I think it
was the question.

00:41:12.971 --> 00:41:13.660
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:41:13.661 --> 00:41:16.210
&gt;&gt; And and then Klemet partly

00:41:16.211 --> 00:41:19.180
answered the question.
And the way.

00:41:19.181 --> 00:41:20.820
&gt;&gt; You can just prove by

00:41:20.821 --> 00:41:22.820
that like in the top version
you're going to

00:41:22.821 --> 00:41:23.820
get exactly what you
saw in the right.

00:41:23.821 --> 00:41:26.820
You only have lists and let
at the beginning of his buddy.

00:41:26.821 --> 00:41:27.820
And few after the [inaudible] .

00:41:27.821 --> 00:41:33.445
&gt;&gt; Right.

00:41:33.446 --> 00:41:34.830
So, okay.

00:41:34.831 --> 00:41:36.630
I guess a short
explanation of why this

00:41:36.631 --> 00:41:39.270
works is with
natural reduction rules,

00:41:39.271 --> 00:41:41.250
we do all the lets
before we start

00:41:41.251 --> 00:41:43.845
building the list, and that's
what makes this working.

00:41:43.846 --> 00:41:45.300
And by the way, you might
worry, hey there's still

00:41:45.301 --> 00:41:46.870
a list left in this program.

00:41:46.871 --> 00:41:49.455
It's not a very
idiomatic C program,

00:41:49.456 --> 00:41:50.670
but we'll actually take

00:41:50.671 --> 00:41:53.040
this operation and compose
it with others in practice,

00:41:53.041 --> 00:41:55.860
and all the lists will reduce
away in a larger program.

00:41:55.861 --> 00:41:59.319
&gt;&gt; Is it [inaudible] to say that

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:04.754
the top version of the program
would require Coq to do

00:42:04.755 --> 00:42:06.845
much like work [inaudible] and

00:42:06.846 --> 00:42:08.000
beginning findings and then

00:42:08.001 --> 00:42:12.000
give you the shape as you
want to get the obvious lists

00:42:12.001 --> 00:42:14.000
in terminal position of

00:42:14.001 --> 00:42:15.460
your programming further
reduce when using other.

00:42:15.461 --> 00:42:20.740
&gt;&gt; I think the answer is
yes. The question was getting

00:42:20.741 --> 00:42:23.115
the top vision to work
nicely in Coq would require

00:42:23.116 --> 00:42:25.950
Coq to do some work
to hoist lets for us.

00:42:25.951 --> 00:42:28.050
We spent a month or
more trying to get

00:42:28.051 --> 00:42:30.345
that to work and it just
wasn't worth the effort.

00:42:30.346 --> 00:42:33.045
So that's why we
switched to CPS.

00:42:33.046 --> 00:42:35.400
And we were trying
to write a proof by

00:42:35.401 --> 00:42:38.045
reflection tactic
that does that for us.

00:42:38.046 --> 00:42:42.225
But this is much nicer

00:42:42.226 --> 00:42:44.250
once you get past
writing everything in

00:42:44.251 --> 00:42:47.143
CPS and proofing it once.

00:42:47.144 --> 00:42:48.560
All right.

00:42:48.561 --> 00:42:50.960
So, one other dimension

00:42:50.961 --> 00:42:53.150
of complication I
should mention is,

00:42:53.151 --> 00:42:55.655
there are two major
implementation strategies for

00:42:55.656 --> 00:42:57.140
arithmetic in this world called

00:42:57.141 --> 00:42:59.405
unsaturated and
saturated arithmetic.

00:42:59.406 --> 00:43:01.250
In unsaturated,
you intentionally

00:43:01.251 --> 00:43:02.690
put digits in
registers that have

00:43:02.691 --> 00:43:04.280
bits to spare beyond what you'll

00:43:04.281 --> 00:43:06.275
actually try to fit in there,

00:43:06.276 --> 00:43:09.350
and then you have to
periodically use currying to

00:43:09.351 --> 00:43:12.605
reduce things down to fit again.

00:43:12.606 --> 00:43:14.870
So our library, to
support this strategy,

00:43:14.871 --> 00:43:16.250
needs to be
parameterized not just

00:43:16.251 --> 00:43:17.855
on digit division strategies,

00:43:17.856 --> 00:43:22.460
but also on when to carry
and between which digit.

00:43:22.461 --> 00:43:23.990
So that's another
parameter when we

00:43:23.991 --> 00:43:26.510
prove correctness
for any such choice.

00:43:26.511 --> 00:43:28.460
Though, key point here,

00:43:28.461 --> 00:43:30.975
we don't prove lack of
overflows once and for all.

00:43:30.976 --> 00:43:32.795
That's going to be
on the next slide

00:43:32.796 --> 00:43:34.675
that comes later in compilation.

00:43:34.676 --> 00:43:37.100
And then the alternative
is saturated arithmetic,

00:43:37.101 --> 00:43:38.410
where we fully utilized to

00:43:38.411 --> 00:43:41.250
register bitwidths
at all times. Yeah?

00:43:41.251 --> 00:43:44.410
&gt;&gt; How do you deal if you
reason using intrinsics,

00:43:44.411 --> 00:43:45.410
like you mentioned earlier,

00:43:45.411 --> 00:43:48.710
for hardware instructions
how dangerous it is.

00:43:48.711 --> 00:43:50.440
Do you have to reason
about other instructions

00:43:50.441 --> 00:43:52.900
that eventually clear the flag?

00:43:52.901 --> 00:43:56.065
Do you have to reason about
interrupts and signals or

00:43:56.066 --> 00:43:57.549
soon that the OS always responds

00:43:57.550 --> 00:43:59.458
every single flag
accepted as before?

00:43:59.459 --> 00:44:02.420
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:44:02.421 --> 00:44:04.795
&gt;&gt; It seems like it's very
new into a very low-level kind

00:44:04.796 --> 00:44:08.030
of reasoning that
might be intriguing,

00:44:08.031 --> 00:44:08.355
I don't know, I'm just curious.

00:44:08.356 --> 00:44:11.320
&gt;&gt; So the question
is, when we depend

00:44:11.321 --> 00:44:13.570
on compiler intrinsics for

00:44:13.571 --> 00:44:15.895
operations like add with carry,

00:44:15.896 --> 00:44:19.540
do we have to worry
that our program gets

00:44:19.541 --> 00:44:21.220
interrupted between
that instruction

00:44:21.221 --> 00:44:23.600
and a later one that
uses the carry bit?

00:44:23.601 --> 00:44:26.850
I think, we assume

00:44:26.851 --> 00:44:28.080
the compiler understands its

00:44:28.081 --> 00:44:29.800
intrinsic well
enough to avoid that.

00:44:29.801 --> 00:44:32.150
The carry that goes into
a regular C variable,

00:44:32.151 --> 00:44:35.580
and so this usual guarantee
should apply there.

00:44:35.581 --> 00:44:38.066
&gt;&gt; Because we don't focus
the processor connect directly.

00:44:38.067 --> 00:44:40.566
&gt;&gt; The ALS will just start
the [inaudible] because

00:44:40.567 --> 00:44:41.566
it's constant for us but-

00:44:41.567 --> 00:44:46.265
&gt;&gt; Well, it is worth
mentioning that

00:44:46.266 --> 00:44:49.130
the popular C compilers often do

00:44:49.131 --> 00:44:51.170
a terrible job of
optimizing programs

00:44:51.171 --> 00:44:53.195
that use carry flags explicitly.

00:44:53.196 --> 00:44:56.450
They like to spill
the carry flags

00:44:56.451 --> 00:44:59.960
into memory all the time.

00:44:59.961 --> 00:45:01.520
It's crazy from the standpoint

00:45:01.521 --> 00:45:03.500
of the assembly code
you'd naturally write.

00:45:03.501 --> 00:45:07.430
So, yeah, but that is
a compiler problem even

00:45:07.431 --> 00:45:08.785
independently in formal methods

00:45:08.786 --> 00:45:09.650
that would be nice to have

00:45:09.651 --> 00:45:12.349
solved in a uniform way.

00:45:12.350 --> 00:45:15.122
Right.

00:45:15.123 --> 00:45:17.090
Yeah.

00:45:17.091 --> 00:45:19.575
I probably said
everything for this slide.

00:45:19.576 --> 00:45:22.310
Okay. So let's actually
start discharging

00:45:22.311 --> 00:45:24.320
this obligation about overflow

00:45:24.321 --> 00:45:25.790
and making sure
we don't have it.

00:45:25.791 --> 00:45:27.620
So imagine in our earlier stages

00:45:27.621 --> 00:45:29.150
we compile this expression,

00:45:29.151 --> 00:45:31.270
which is a somewhat optimized
version of some sort of

00:45:31.271 --> 00:45:34.040
program as for
variables x and y.

00:45:34.041 --> 00:45:38.300
And what we do at this point
in our pipeline is,

00:45:38.301 --> 00:45:40.520
we reify this to a syntax tree

00:45:40.521 --> 00:45:41.870
in a type defined inside of

00:45:41.871 --> 00:45:43.800
Coq with a formal
semantics looks.

00:45:43.801 --> 00:45:46.460
And then we do

00:45:46.461 --> 00:45:48.290
some constant folding
to reduce it as much as

00:45:48.291 --> 00:45:49.730
possible while we still have

00:45:49.731 --> 00:45:53.045
this nice algebraic style
of expression.

00:45:53.046 --> 00:45:55.760
Then we flatten it into
a forum where we introduce

00:45:55.761 --> 00:45:58.010
a name for every
intermediate computation.

00:45:58.011 --> 00:46:01.460
That's roughly the level
we were looking at before.

00:46:01.461 --> 00:46:02.735
And by the way,

00:46:02.736 --> 00:46:03.500
there might have already been

00:46:03.501 --> 00:46:04.760
some lets in the starting point,

00:46:04.761 --> 00:46:07.685
but I'm just assuming
we didn't have any here.

00:46:07.686 --> 00:46:10.700
And now, we're going to
infer bounds and this is

00:46:10.701 --> 00:46:13.043
just abstract interpretation
with intervals,

00:46:13.044 --> 00:46:14.495
there are a few twists.

00:46:14.496 --> 00:46:15.860
So we assume bounds for

00:46:15.861 --> 00:46:17.000
the all the free variables

00:46:17.001 --> 00:46:18.140
in the term and
we're just going to

00:46:18.141 --> 00:46:19.680
push those through
all the operations and

00:46:19.681 --> 00:46:22.350
infer bounds for
all the intermediate terms.

00:46:22.351 --> 00:46:25.535
And then, based on
the bounds we've inferred,

00:46:25.536 --> 00:46:29.225
we can see, let's see C
needs at least 52 bits,

00:46:29.226 --> 00:46:34.445
but the upper bound is
adding a few powers of 2,

00:46:34.446 --> 00:46:36.665
but that's a relief.

00:46:36.666 --> 00:46:38.525
That's still less
than two to the 64.

00:46:38.526 --> 00:46:41.420
And D similar sort
of thing happens.

00:46:41.421 --> 00:46:44.030
So we can put both of
these in a 64-bit integers.

00:46:44.031 --> 00:46:46.160
And the main pain

00:46:46.161 --> 00:46:47.990
for us in implementing
this analysis was

00:46:47.991 --> 00:46:50.570
proving bounds for
bitwise operators

00:46:50.571 --> 00:46:52.543
on negative numbers
for what it's worth.

00:46:52.544 --> 00:46:53.810
But we had to do
something sound.

00:46:53.811 --> 00:46:55.160
In that case, we do need bitwise

00:46:55.161 --> 00:46:56.420
operators on
negative numbers for

00:46:56.421 --> 00:46:59.686
popular algorithms to
get the best performance.

00:46:59.687 --> 00:47:05.380
So we do this analysis
and it pops out some code.

00:47:05.381 --> 00:47:07.130
And by the way, we're

00:47:07.131 --> 00:47:10.665
generating ASCIIs in Coq
for this final phase.

00:47:10.666 --> 00:47:13.190
But we want a pretty print
to the C. And

00:47:13.191 --> 00:47:15.945
the way we do that is JSON.

00:47:15.946 --> 00:47:17.720
One of the students working
on this project wrote

00:47:17.721 --> 00:47:20.060
a script that registers
something like

00:47:20.061 --> 00:47:21.890
a thousand different Coq macros

00:47:21.891 --> 00:47:23.495
so that Coq itself pretty prints

00:47:23.496 --> 00:47:28.545
its own code as C. It's
very worth black magicy.

00:47:28.546 --> 00:47:30.260
And then we feed that

00:47:30.261 --> 00:47:34.575
to normal C compilers
to finish the process.

00:47:34.576 --> 00:47:37.390
So other parts that
don't have time to

00:47:37.391 --> 00:47:39.175
go into more detail about,

00:47:39.176 --> 00:47:41.170
but remember early on
I said we start out

00:47:41.171 --> 00:47:43.480
with two dimensional points
for elliptic curves and we

00:47:43.481 --> 00:47:45.040
reason about replacing them with

00:47:45.041 --> 00:47:46.630
four dimensional points
and probably

00:47:46.631 --> 00:47:49.300
we have other examples
that are even fancier.

00:47:49.301 --> 00:47:51.520
We built some new Coq tactics
to justify all that,

00:47:51.521 --> 00:47:53.805
and we proved all the
popular point formats

00:47:53.806 --> 00:47:55.570
something like 10 of them.

00:47:55.571 --> 00:47:57.490
And they're mostly
automatic proofs

00:47:57.491 --> 00:47:58.540
after a few months spent

00:47:58.541 --> 00:47:59.920
on basically a tactic that

00:47:59.921 --> 00:48:01.745
does things computer
algebra systems do,

00:48:01.746 --> 00:48:03.715
but improve generating way.

00:48:03.716 --> 00:48:05.290
And we have a whole menu

00:48:05.291 --> 00:48:06.610
of arithmetic
operations that are in

00:48:06.611 --> 00:48:09.250
this library including finding
inverses, exponentiation,

00:48:09.251 --> 00:48:11.200
square roots, and going out of

00:48:11.201 --> 00:48:12.520
our strange encodings back

00:48:12.521 --> 00:48:14.730
into standard wire
format encodings.

00:48:14.731 --> 00:48:16.570
And several of these
have their own dimensions

00:48:16.571 --> 00:48:18.040
of choices from small menus of

00:48:18.041 --> 00:48:21.100
operations like
exponentiation or

00:48:21.101 --> 00:48:22.350
square roots in particular,

00:48:22.351 --> 00:48:24.725
there are like two different
recipes for square roots

00:48:24.726 --> 00:48:26.040
depending on the number

00:48:26.041 --> 00:48:27.430
theoretic properties
of your prime.

00:48:27.431 --> 00:48:29.050
So we define both of those and

00:48:29.051 --> 00:48:30.880
you can pick which one
you want without

00:48:30.881 --> 00:48:34.540
having to worry about breaking
the soundness. All right.

00:48:34.541 --> 00:48:36.940
So how did we evaluate

00:48:36.941 --> 00:48:39.775
the actual performance
effectiveness of this approach?

00:48:39.776 --> 00:48:41.830
Well, first, I'll
say we have about

00:48:41.831 --> 00:48:44.275
38,000 lines of code
in our full library.

00:48:44.276 --> 00:48:45.730
About half of that is

00:48:45.731 --> 00:48:47.320
generic math stuff that would

00:48:47.321 --> 00:48:49.885
fit in the Coq standard
library conceptually.

00:48:49.886 --> 00:48:51.970
Very little work,
very little code is

00:48:51.971 --> 00:48:53.770
needed to instantiate
to a new prime modules,

00:48:53.771 --> 00:48:55.495
and I'll show an experiment
justifying that.

00:48:55.496 --> 00:48:56.830
In particular, we wrote

00:48:56.831 --> 00:48:59.110
a Python script under
3,000 lines of code that

00:48:59.111 --> 00:49:00.820
examines your prime number

00:49:00.821 --> 00:49:03.760
and makes it guess about
the best parameters.

00:49:03.761 --> 00:49:05.470
And importantly,
the prime number needs to

00:49:05.471 --> 00:49:07.345
be written in a
suggestive form like this,

00:49:07.346 --> 00:49:08.800
where the actual way
you break it into

00:49:08.801 --> 00:49:10.865
additions and subtractions
with powers of two,

00:49:10.866 --> 00:49:12.610
influences how the
heuristic works.

00:49:12.611 --> 00:49:13.840
But this is the way crypto

00:49:13.841 --> 00:49:15.640
implementation people are
used to writing things,

00:49:15.641 --> 00:49:17.650
so that they can
sort of look into it

00:49:17.651 --> 00:49:20.225
and see the essence of
the right optimizations to use.

00:49:20.226 --> 00:49:22.120
And this script that generates

00:49:22.121 --> 00:49:24.084
the parameters is not trusted.

00:49:24.085 --> 00:49:26.560
The worst thing that
happens if it's wrong is

00:49:26.561 --> 00:49:29.110
the compilation might time out.

00:49:29.111 --> 00:49:30.886
If compilation finishes,

00:49:30.887 --> 00:49:32.545
you're guaranteed
to get correct code.

00:49:32.546 --> 00:49:34.435
Might not be efficient code,

00:49:34.436 --> 00:49:36.400
but you can auto tune with

00:49:36.401 --> 00:49:38.140
many different parameters
and pick the ones that

00:49:38.141 --> 00:49:40.940
finish and actually give
you performant code.

00:49:40.941 --> 00:49:43.105
So, we did a little bit of that.

00:49:43.106 --> 00:49:45.040
We needed to get a list of

00:49:45.041 --> 00:49:47.890
different moduli to
try for arithmetic.

00:49:47.891 --> 00:49:49.150
So what we did was,

00:49:49.151 --> 00:49:50.440
we scraped the most popular

00:49:50.441 --> 00:49:52.060
mailing list for
elliptic curves,

00:49:52.061 --> 00:49:54.160
and we processed the text

00:49:54.161 --> 00:49:56.420
and found everything
that was a prime number.

00:49:56.421 --> 00:50:00.130
And that gave us about 80
different prime numbers.

00:50:00.131 --> 00:50:01.870
And luckily, only a few of them

00:50:01.871 --> 00:50:03.550
turned out to be
complete nonsense posted

00:50:03.551 --> 00:50:05.110
by people who were new to

00:50:05.111 --> 00:50:07.027
the mailing list and didn't
understand elliptic curves.

00:50:07.028 --> 00:50:08.890
But those with time out when
we tried to compile them.

00:50:08.891 --> 00:50:11.778
There were maybe like three
of those or something.

00:50:11.779 --> 00:50:13.535
So then, we automatically built

00:50:13.536 --> 00:50:15.770
both 64-bit and
32-bit versions for

00:50:15.771 --> 00:50:18.020
all these primes as well
as building versions for

00:50:18.021 --> 00:50:19.955
the two main
arithmetic strategies:

00:50:19.956 --> 00:50:21.555
saturated and unsaturated.

00:50:21.556 --> 00:50:23.065
And then we would just
free the platform,

00:50:23.066 --> 00:50:24.460
we'd keep the faster one.

00:50:24.461 --> 00:50:26.685
And so that leads
to these results.

00:50:26.686 --> 00:50:30.130
Here are the ones for
64-bit arithmetic.

00:50:30.131 --> 00:50:32.300
It was kind of hard to figure

00:50:32.301 --> 00:50:34.445
out what are we bench
marking against as a baseline

00:50:34.446 --> 00:50:39.340
because most prime
field arithmetic

00:50:39.341 --> 00:50:41.005
is specialized to
particular algorithms,

00:50:41.006 --> 00:50:42.315
but what we decided
to do is take

00:50:42.316 --> 00:50:45.460
the new multi precision
integer library,

00:50:45.461 --> 00:50:49.050
which is probably the most
popular generic big number

00:50:49.051 --> 00:50:50.725
C library out there,

00:50:50.726 --> 00:50:53.410
and see how much faster you
can get your code to be by

00:50:53.411 --> 00:50:54.520
using our library versus

00:50:54.521 --> 00:50:56.260
using this
off-the-shelf C library.

00:50:56.261 --> 00:50:57.560
Where we put the C code,

00:50:57.561 --> 00:50:59.255
we'd recompile it with a macro,

00:50:59.256 --> 00:51:01.585
but a pre-processor macro,
set to the prime number.

00:51:01.586 --> 00:51:03.505
But otherwise, it was

00:51:03.506 --> 00:51:05.320
mostly run-time specialization

00:51:05.321 --> 00:51:06.450
to different prime numbers.

00:51:06.451 --> 00:51:08.830
But this library, in
the last few years,

00:51:08.831 --> 00:51:10.060
I think did start doing

00:51:10.061 --> 00:51:12.340
compile time code generation
to specialized things,

00:51:12.341 --> 00:51:15.134
the module ISO, it does
take advantage of that.

00:51:15.135 --> 00:51:18.960
So the bottom line
here is what we get.

00:51:18.961 --> 00:51:22.840
The x-axis is the number of
bits in the prime modulus.

00:51:22.841 --> 00:51:25.360
And then these two
other curves here

00:51:25.361 --> 00:51:28.075
for two versions
of the GMP library.

00:51:28.076 --> 00:51:29.440
One, where we say you

00:51:29.441 --> 00:51:31.210
must produce
constant time code that

00:51:31.211 --> 00:51:34.735
does not leak off secret
data values through timing,

00:51:34.736 --> 00:51:36.035
and the other when
we say, "All right.

00:51:36.036 --> 00:51:38.785
Feel free to use optimizations
that break constant time."

00:51:38.786 --> 00:51:40.085
Which is not okay for crypto,

00:51:40.086 --> 00:51:42.455
but which gives that library
an inherent advantage.

00:51:42.456 --> 00:51:44.410
And you can see, even
for this bottom curve,

00:51:44.411 --> 00:51:46.150
which is allowed to
break constant time,

00:51:46.151 --> 00:51:48.970
we were ranging from
between about one and a half

00:51:48.971 --> 00:51:52.150
times faster to 10 times
faster, in some cases.

00:51:52.151 --> 00:51:53.725
And this is
completely automatic.

00:51:53.726 --> 00:51:56.350
A script scrapes all the primes
and does this for you.

00:51:56.351 --> 00:51:59.640
So no new coding work,
no new proof work.

00:51:59.641 --> 00:52:02.770
And also, we run
the same thing for 32-bit code,

00:52:02.771 --> 00:52:04.885
which you run on
an Android phone

00:52:04.886 --> 00:52:08.034
and get a pretty
similar kinds of results

00:52:08.035 --> 00:52:10.800
going up to a neighborhood
of 10 times faster

00:52:10.801 --> 00:52:13.723
for many of these curves
compared to the GMP.

00:52:13.724 --> 00:52:18.080
&gt;&gt; So what the graph seems to

00:52:18.081 --> 00:52:20.315
indicate is that GMP is smart

00:52:20.316 --> 00:52:22.860
only when the most of this
is about working work size.

00:52:22.861 --> 00:52:26.530
Right. Will JSON take that?

00:52:26.531 --> 00:52:29.530
Like around one many two
and nothing wrong around

00:52:29.531 --> 00:52:32.240
that six et cetera.

00:52:32.241 --> 00:52:33.660
&gt;&gt; That sounds right,

00:52:33.661 --> 00:52:35.775
but then also
apparently were seeing.

00:52:35.776 --> 00:52:36.850
So the suggestion was,

00:52:36.851 --> 00:52:39.040
we look for four points on

00:52:39.041 --> 00:52:42.693
the X-axis that are multiples
of the base word size.

00:52:42.694 --> 00:52:46.075
The interesting thing is.

00:52:46.076 --> 00:52:50.385
Let's see. You're basically,

00:52:50.386 --> 00:52:54.340
pointing out these
discontinuity is out there.

00:52:54.341 --> 00:52:56.006
&gt;&gt; One of them about 512.

00:52:54.342 --> 00:52:59.274
&gt;&gt; Okay. Yeah, that can be true.

00:52:59.275 --> 00:53:00.760
What stands out for me are

00:53:00.761 --> 00:53:02.650
these points where suddenly
our code does a lot worse.

00:53:02.651 --> 00:53:05.278
I'm not sure what's
happening there.

00:53:05.279 --> 00:53:07.923
There's another question?

00:53:07.924 --> 00:53:08.923
&gt;&gt; Oh, yeah.

00:53:08.924 --> 00:53:10.660
I was just wondering
why in the bottom one

00:53:10.661 --> 00:53:13.660
the non-constant times
are slower.

00:53:13.661 --> 00:53:14.385
&gt;&gt; Yeah, that's kind of weird.

00:53:14.386 --> 00:53:15.800
We're not sure why that is.

00:53:15.801 --> 00:53:17.715
It's probably just, so

00:53:17.716 --> 00:53:19.910
the question is why are
the non-constant time ones

00:53:19.911 --> 00:53:25.095
often faster or slower than
the constant time ones?

00:53:25.096 --> 00:53:27.110
Probably just because GMP

00:53:27.111 --> 00:53:29.380
isn't tested on
Android phones too much.

00:53:29.381 --> 00:53:31.220
We had to work hard enough
to get it to build that

00:53:31.221 --> 00:53:33.525
I can sort of believe
that isn't happening much.

00:53:33.526 --> 00:53:35.915
We couldn't get C++ version
to build for instance,

00:53:35.916 --> 00:53:40.450
within our patient's budget.

00:53:40.451 --> 00:53:43.570
All right. So this is,

00:53:43.571 --> 00:53:45.475
like, all the primes we
found on this mailing list.

00:53:45.476 --> 00:53:47.080
There are also a few primes

00:53:47.081 --> 00:53:48.310
that are really
important practice,

00:53:48.311 --> 00:53:50.470
where we wanted to compare
against not just GMP

00:53:50.471 --> 00:53:53.380
but also the best
assembly code out there.

00:53:53.381 --> 00:53:57.970
So here is part of the
Curve25519 codebase you'd need

00:53:57.971 --> 00:53:59.745
for TLS 1.3 for

00:53:59.746 --> 00:54:02.740
the preferred cipher suites
at least. Here's our code.

00:54:02.741 --> 00:54:05.150
I'll explain why there
are two lines for it.

00:54:05.151 --> 00:54:06.010
We're basically listing

00:54:06.011 --> 00:54:07.270
these different
implementations by

00:54:07.271 --> 00:54:08.440
how many CPU cycles they

00:54:08.441 --> 00:54:11.995
take and our code
is doing all right.

00:54:11.996 --> 00:54:13.835
The generated C, which is

00:54:13.836 --> 00:54:15.430
the most literal
interpretation of

00:54:15.431 --> 00:54:17.013
the pipeline I've
been talking about,

00:54:17.014 --> 00:54:18.430
was something like 20% off from

00:54:18.431 --> 00:54:21.000
the best assembly code
to start out with.

00:54:21.001 --> 00:54:23.230
Now, what we did to try
to understand what we were

00:54:23.231 --> 00:54:26.365
missing to be faster
is we looked at,

00:54:26.366 --> 00:54:27.760
I think it was this C program,

00:54:27.761 --> 00:54:29.430
which is the best
known C program.

00:54:29.431 --> 00:54:30.918
Or maybe we looked
directly at the assembly,

00:54:30.919 --> 00:54:32.755
I'm not sure, and then,

00:54:32.756 --> 00:54:33.940
took the code that someone

00:54:33.941 --> 00:54:35.050
else had written and translated

00:54:35.051 --> 00:54:37.450
into CalK in the same form
as a functional program,

00:54:37.451 --> 00:54:39.520
and then proved that that form

00:54:39.521 --> 00:54:42.160
is equal to ours extentionally.

00:54:42.161 --> 00:54:44.075
And so that got us,

00:54:44.076 --> 00:54:47.098
like, 10% better performance.

00:54:47.099 --> 00:54:49.300
And our conclusion
from all that is that,

00:54:49.301 --> 00:54:50.620
what we're really missing is

00:54:50.621 --> 00:54:53.350
good instruction scheduling
and register allocation,

00:54:53.351 --> 00:54:54.955
which the experts are doing

00:54:54.956 --> 00:54:56.676
by hand as they write this code.

00:54:56.677 --> 00:54:58.780
So that's one of our directions
for future work to

00:54:58.781 --> 00:55:01.120
build a compiler that does
those in the right way.

00:55:01.121 --> 00:55:03.700
It turns out Clang and GCC

00:55:03.701 --> 00:55:05.170
actually do really bad

00:55:05.171 --> 00:55:06.820
register allocation
for this kind of code.

00:55:06.821 --> 00:55:07.960
And you can get
a factor of two in

00:55:07.961 --> 00:55:09.580
performance in many cases by

00:55:09.581 --> 00:55:11.350
handwriting the
assembly code and doing

00:55:11.351 --> 00:55:13.720
instruction scheduling and
register allocation yourself.

00:55:13.721 --> 00:55:17.080
But it would be nice to
have a compiler for that.

00:55:17.081 --> 00:55:22.350
We also measured performance
of our P256 implementation.

00:55:22.351 --> 00:55:24.970
This is the most common
elliptic curve in TLS,

00:55:24.971 --> 00:55:26.440
significantly
harder to implement

00:55:26.441 --> 00:55:28.334
efficiently on
commodity processors,

00:55:28.335 --> 00:55:30.300
which is why the other one
was invented.

00:55:30.301 --> 00:55:33.310
But here, you can see
we're like, roughly,

00:55:33.311 --> 00:55:35.645
twice the number of CPU cycles

00:55:35.646 --> 00:55:37.950
versus the best
known assembly code.

00:55:37.951 --> 00:55:40.090
And on this slide
and the previous one,

00:55:40.091 --> 00:55:41.730
like everyone else who
works in the space,

00:55:41.731 --> 00:55:42.940
we at least beat
the performance of

00:55:42.941 --> 00:55:44.860
OpenSSL cross-platform C code,

00:55:44.861 --> 00:55:47.170
which sounds impressive
at first until you realize

00:55:47.171 --> 00:55:50.570
no one uses this code in
practice because it's too slow.

00:55:50.571 --> 00:55:54.595
But we're at least reasonable
against that baseline.

00:55:54.596 --> 00:55:56.020
All right.

00:55:56.021 --> 00:55:59.075
So the next steps you
want to tackle here,

00:55:59.076 --> 00:56:01.150
one I was just mentioning is,

00:56:01.151 --> 00:56:03.835
some of the last phases
of compilation

00:56:03.836 --> 00:56:06.190
really deserve
good compiler support

00:56:06.191 --> 00:56:07.660
even if we forget about proofs.

00:56:07.661 --> 00:56:09.160
Namely, we want better register

00:56:09.161 --> 00:56:10.660
allocation and
instruction scheduling.

00:56:10.661 --> 00:56:13.455
So we don't have to hand-modify

00:56:13.456 --> 00:56:16.030
the last stages of what
pops out of our compiler.

00:56:16.031 --> 00:56:17.830
And we think it's okay to write

00:56:17.831 --> 00:56:19.840
some sort of really
expensive, maybe,

00:56:19.841 --> 00:56:22.055
random search-based version
of this that you let

00:56:22.056 --> 00:56:25.420
run overnight for your
important crypto-algorithms.

00:56:25.421 --> 00:56:26.770
We'd like to expand our scope

00:56:26.771 --> 00:56:28.340
to more of the crypto
primitives from

00:56:28.341 --> 00:56:31.525
TLS and other protocols
and, in general,

00:56:31.526 --> 00:56:35.260
try to do more of the parts
of TLS inside CalK with

00:56:35.261 --> 00:56:37.090
code derivation-based
strategies as

00:56:37.091 --> 00:56:40.390
opposed to write-the-code-and-verify-it
strategies.

00:56:40.391 --> 00:56:41.980
And there also seem to be

00:56:41.981 --> 00:56:44.110
good matches with
other kinds of crypto,

00:56:44.111 --> 00:56:45.730
especially emerging kinds like

00:56:45.731 --> 00:56:47.830
lattice-based cryptography
or, apparently,

00:56:47.831 --> 00:56:49.750
there's still a lot of
experimentation about

00:56:49.751 --> 00:56:52.080
how we should use lattices
and crypto primitives.

00:56:52.081 --> 00:56:53.380
So it would be nice
to have a compiler

00:56:53.381 --> 00:56:54.850
that takes each
such new idea and

00:56:54.851 --> 00:56:56.015
automatically builds fast code

00:56:56.016 --> 00:56:58.925
for all the platforms
that are out there.

00:56:58.926 --> 00:57:02.940
And I think I'll stop
here with our GitHub URL,

00:57:02.941 --> 00:57:05.296
where you can download
this and give it a try.

00:57:05.297 --> 00:57:11.590
Thanks.

00:57:11.591 --> 00:57:13.400
&gt;&gt; So why do you wanna

00:57:13.401 --> 00:57:15.800
search for a good
register allocation?

00:57:15.801 --> 00:57:18.040
Presumably those
rock star coders are

00:57:18.041 --> 00:57:20.961
not running searches over
spaces in their heads.

00:57:20.962 --> 00:57:22.660
They have heuristics in mind

00:57:22.661 --> 00:57:24.673
and they're applying
those heuristics.

00:57:24.674 --> 00:57:26.120
Presumably, if you talk

00:57:26.121 --> 00:57:27.623
to them and learn
these heuristics,

00:57:27.624 --> 00:57:28.250
you apply them just like

00:57:28.251 --> 00:57:30.716
you're applying
other heuristics.

00:57:30.717 --> 00:57:32.100
&gt;&gt; We tried that
a little bit so far.

00:57:32.101 --> 00:57:34.590
Well, maybe we should try more.

00:57:34.591 --> 00:57:37.260
The question was, why

00:57:37.261 --> 00:57:38.880
don't we just talk to
the people who are handwriting

00:57:38.881 --> 00:57:40.200
the code to get the heuristics

00:57:40.201 --> 00:57:41.520
out of their brains
that we've put into

00:57:41.521 --> 00:57:46.350
compilers for the register
allocation and similar stuff.

00:57:46.351 --> 00:57:48.570
I don't think the experts
have the level of

00:57:48.571 --> 00:57:49.890
introspective knowledge to just

00:57:49.891 --> 00:57:51.300
tell us what the algorithm is.

00:57:51.301 --> 00:57:52.915
We haven't been successful yet

00:57:52.916 --> 00:57:56.280
at extracting it but
we'll keep trying.

00:57:56.281 --> 00:57:57.565
At the moment I'm
betting more on

00:57:57.566 --> 00:57:59.842
random search based approaches
being effective.

00:57:59.843 --> 00:58:02.763
We'll see. Yeah.

00:58:02.764 --> 00:58:05.530
&gt;&gt; Can you say a word
about vector instructions?

00:58:05.531 --> 00:58:07.150
&gt;&gt; Vector instructions
have been on our list to

00:58:07.151 --> 00:58:10.860
support since the start.

00:58:10.861 --> 00:58:13.510
We don't know how
it'll work exactly.

00:58:13.511 --> 00:58:16.260
We'd like to do some
manipulations at the level of

00:58:16.261 --> 00:58:18.165
functional programs that find

00:58:18.166 --> 00:58:19.905
appropriate units
to group together,

00:58:19.906 --> 00:58:24.115
but no concrete insights
to share at this time.

00:58:24.116 --> 00:58:25.170
It is important to get

00:58:25.171 --> 00:58:27.660
the best performance on
particular processors but we

00:58:27.661 --> 00:58:32.556
haven't gotten there yet.

00:58:32.557 --> 00:58:33.180
&gt;&gt; Do you have
any insights as to

00:58:33.181 --> 00:58:34.800
why the Intel
compiler is so much

00:58:34.801 --> 00:58:39.080
faster than your compiler
and your licensed compilers?

00:58:39.081 --> 00:58:41.820
&gt;&gt; I think it might be
handling of Carry flags.

00:58:41.821 --> 00:58:43.640
GCC likes to spill
Carry flags to

00:58:43.641 --> 00:58:48.504
the stack and the Intel
compiler is reasonable.

00:58:48.505 --> 00:58:49.680
&gt;&gt; So there's really
two things going

00:58:49.681 --> 00:58:51.435
on here based on verified part,

00:58:51.436 --> 00:58:53.265
which is, of course, I
think it's very impressive.

00:58:53.266 --> 00:58:54.752
There's also
the automation part,

00:58:54.753 --> 00:58:56.230
if you're doing things
automatically that

00:58:56.231 --> 00:58:58.711
people are doing manually.

00:58:58.712 --> 00:58:59.725
So I'm a bit

00:58:59.726 --> 00:59:01.410
curious when people
do this manually,

00:59:01.411 --> 00:59:02.025
I think they do it manually.

00:59:02.026 --> 00:59:07.560
Like, why don't they run this kind
of program to get [inaudible]?

00:59:07.561 --> 00:59:09.420
&gt;&gt; So the question is,
why weren't the experts

00:59:09.421 --> 00:59:11.070
already scripting the work

00:59:11.071 --> 00:59:12.135
that we're doing automatically?

00:59:12.136 --> 00:59:14.665
Even without the proofs,
it's still a big advantage.

00:59:14.666 --> 00:59:16.785
And the reason is

00:59:16.786 --> 00:59:19.630
they didn't think it
could be done, I guess.

00:59:19.631 --> 00:59:21.120
When we talk to
the people who've

00:59:21.121 --> 00:59:22.965
designed all these protocols,

00:59:22.966 --> 00:59:25.830
these primitives, they're pretty

00:59:25.831 --> 00:59:28.120
surprised that you
can generalize.

00:59:28.121 --> 00:59:29.995
It's just a functional
program that

00:59:29.996 --> 00:59:32.550
embodies all the algorithms
they've been figuring out.

00:59:32.551 --> 00:59:34.573
&gt;&gt; They can do that. It
seems completely considerable

00:59:34.574 --> 00:59:37.573
that you could do much
better than they do.

00:59:37.574 --> 00:59:42.057
Just saying that you can unroll
the loop ten times and do

00:59:42.058 --> 00:59:45.057
this over ten integrations.

00:59:45.058 --> 00:59:45.630
But they can't do it manually
because it's too much work.

00:59:45.631 --> 00:59:47.850
&gt;&gt; Well, they already do
that, even in OpenSSL,

00:59:47.851 --> 00:59:50.840
and Vale does
that kind of unrolling.

00:59:50.841 --> 00:59:51.430
&gt;&gt; [inaudible] unrolling since

00:59:51.431 --> 00:59:52.810
the loop thing then
they can't do it.

00:59:52.811 --> 00:59:56.260
At some point they're going
to beat the [inaudible].

00:59:56.261 --> 00:59:58.200
&gt;&gt; Well, they just
write scripts that do

00:59:58.201 --> 01:00:00.050
the loop unrolling for them.

01:00:00.051 --> 01:00:00.600
&gt;&gt; But they have scripts.

01:00:00.601 --> 01:00:02.430
&gt;&gt; That part works.
But as far as I know,

01:00:02.431 --> 01:00:05.130
there are no scripts prior
to ours that are generalized

01:00:05.131 --> 01:00:06.870
in either the Prime modulus

01:00:06.871 --> 01:00:08.960
or the target
hardware architecture.

01:00:08.961 --> 01:00:11.340
They get rewritten for each
one or at least they're

01:00:11.341 --> 01:00:14.560
significant implementation
effort for each new variation.

01:00:14.561 --> 01:00:17.502
&gt;&gt; So it seems like it could,

01:00:17.503 --> 01:00:17.708
at some point, beat
the automation aspect too and

01:00:17.709 --> 01:00:21.270
do it faster than.

01:00:21.271 --> 01:00:23.718
&gt;&gt; I think maybe there's
these two points.

01:00:23.719 --> 01:00:29.440
Maybe it's because
you have proofs,

01:00:29.441 --> 01:00:33.270
you are emboldened to
actually search for the stuff.

01:00:33.271 --> 01:00:35.335
Because if you just wrote
a script and you just,

01:00:35.336 --> 01:00:37.515
like, here is
the implementation,

01:00:37.516 --> 01:00:39.615
and it happens to be faster than

01:00:39.616 --> 01:00:42.274
the other one on
some test vector,

01:00:42.275 --> 01:00:43.660
how do you know
that it's actually

01:00:43.661 --> 01:00:46.984
a reasonable implementation?

01:00:46.985 --> 01:00:51.911
So I think that the proofs
are an enabler here.

01:00:51.912 --> 01:00:53.015
The other point is, I think,

01:00:53.016 --> 01:00:54.270
so Haskell Star is also

01:00:54.271 --> 01:00:59.360
doing this kind of
metaprogramming to generate

01:00:59.361 --> 01:01:02.055
implementations that
are parameterized by

01:01:02.056 --> 01:01:07.417
a template for the
representation of the big name.

01:01:07.418 --> 01:01:09.120
I think what you're doing that's

01:01:09.121 --> 01:01:11.070
maybe more than
what "Haskell" is

01:01:11.071 --> 01:01:16.575
doing is that Haskell drives

01:01:16.576 --> 01:01:19.440
all the operations except

01:01:19.441 --> 01:01:24.394
the modular reduction
generically via metaprogramming.

01:01:24.395 --> 01:01:28.025
And then you have to provide
just the modular reduction

01:01:28.026 --> 01:01:32.054
and you do the modular
reduction as well.

01:01:32.055 --> 01:01:36.065
But I think Haskell has,
in Haskell's defense,

01:01:36.066 --> 01:01:39.725
they ensure that
they always stay

01:01:39.726 --> 01:01:45.230
in the particular template
that was provided, whereas,

01:01:45.231 --> 01:01:47.570
given the remark
that the discussion

01:01:47.571 --> 01:01:50.340
that we had, I think yours,

01:01:50.341 --> 01:01:52.970
in the intermediate states,

01:01:52.971 --> 01:01:54.405
you may actually depart from

01:01:54.406 --> 01:01:57.250
the original template that

01:01:57.251 --> 01:02:00.050
the implementer may
have had in mind.

01:02:00.051 --> 01:02:03.210
&gt;&gt; Right. So just to repeat
both the previous remarks for

01:02:03.211 --> 01:02:06.805
people following along at
home and this other remark.

01:02:06.806 --> 01:02:09.090
First one was having proofs of

01:02:09.091 --> 01:02:11.810
correctness emboldens
the programmer to try

01:02:11.811 --> 01:02:13.170
new generation strategies and

01:02:13.171 --> 01:02:14.280
maybe people who didn't have

01:02:14.281 --> 01:02:15.540
proofs in the bad
old days didn't

01:02:15.541 --> 01:02:17.530
try these strategies
for that reason.

01:02:17.531 --> 01:02:19.245
The second remark was that

01:02:19.246 --> 01:02:21.896
the Haskell star library

01:02:21.897 --> 01:02:24.960
is generic over
the Prime modulus except in

01:02:24.961 --> 01:02:27.910
the modular reduction code
and is set up

01:02:27.911 --> 01:02:31.290
to guarantee in
the appropriate sense

01:02:31.291 --> 01:02:32.745
that you stick in
the representation

01:02:32.746 --> 01:02:35.015
that the implementer would want.

01:02:35.016 --> 01:02:38.905
And it's true that our library
does not guarantee that.

01:02:38.906 --> 01:02:41.160
And again I'll just

01:02:41.161 --> 01:02:42.600
mention all the cases where

01:02:42.601 --> 01:02:44.055
you're outside
that representation

01:02:44.056 --> 01:02:45.570
only occur at
compile time and get

01:02:45.571 --> 01:02:47.760
reduced away to cases
where you do stay

01:02:47.761 --> 01:02:49.650
inside that representation as

01:02:49.651 --> 01:02:50.840
long as you chose
the right parameters

01:02:50.841 --> 01:02:55.296
but you might not have.

01:02:55.297 --> 01:02:57.720
&gt;&gt; Are you planning
to go to assembly?

01:02:57.721 --> 01:02:59.040
&gt;&gt; The question is, are we
planning to go to assembly?

01:02:59.041 --> 01:03:01.410
Yes. We are writing

01:03:01.411 --> 01:03:03.540
a new verified compiler for

01:03:03.541 --> 01:03:05.100
a C-like language that we

01:03:05.101 --> 01:03:07.890
are planning to
connect into this.

01:03:07.891 --> 01:03:10.860
It's pretty easy to

01:03:10.861 --> 01:03:12.180
do that for this code

01:03:12.181 --> 01:03:13.685
because it's very close
to assembly already,

01:03:13.686 --> 01:03:14.850
but we want to expand

01:03:14.851 --> 01:03:16.560
the scope to include
loops and other things

01:03:16.561 --> 01:03:20.507
that some of those related
projects have already tackled.

01:03:20.508 --> 01:03:22.780
Yes.

01:03:22.781 --> 01:03:24.246
&gt;&gt; But why do you need

01:03:22.782 --> 01:03:23.868
the mailing list to
search for primes?

01:03:23.869 --> 01:03:26.870
You could search for
primes yourself and maybe

01:03:26.871 --> 01:03:29.145
find better ones that
anyone has even thought of.

01:03:29.146 --> 01:03:32.830
&gt;&gt; We can choose
random parameters to

01:03:32.831 --> 01:03:34.390
cryptographic algorithms
and hope we hit

01:03:34.391 --> 01:03:37.860
on something useful. Yes.

01:03:37.861 --> 01:03:39.070
&gt;&gt; Ones that are close to.

01:03:39.071 --> 01:03:41.890
If it was up to you, you
don't think [inaudible].

01:03:41.891 --> 01:03:43.945
&gt;&gt; I don't actually know
what goes into deciding

01:03:43.946 --> 01:03:46.865
which one of these primes
is the right one to use.

01:03:46.866 --> 01:03:48.340
But I think it can
be a huge mistake

01:03:48.341 --> 01:03:49.990
to find the ones with
the right operations are

01:03:49.991 --> 01:03:51.460
fast because you want

01:03:51.461 --> 01:03:55.695
cracking your
cryptography to be slow.

01:03:55.696 --> 01:04:01.385
&gt;&gt; You have to know
which ones aren't safe?

01:04:01.386 --> 01:04:03.189
I mean there's algorithms for

01:04:03.190 --> 01:04:03.626
determining whether
they're prime,

01:04:03.627 --> 01:04:09.630
but there are other traps
and [inaudible].

01:04:09.631 --> 01:04:10.650
&gt;&gt; The hard part
here is you need

01:04:10.651 --> 01:04:12.600
to find parameters where

01:04:12.601 --> 01:04:14.310
the right operations are

01:04:14.311 --> 01:04:17.470
fast and where certain other
operations are really slow.

01:04:17.471 --> 01:04:19.830
And it's hard to use

01:04:19.831 --> 01:04:22.779
testing to ensure
things are really slow.

01:04:22.780 --> 01:04:27.312
&gt;&gt; What where the
bad primes that

01:04:27.313 --> 01:04:27.902
the [inaudible] were
posted on the list?

01:04:27.903 --> 01:04:30.540
What was bad about them?

01:04:30.541 --> 01:04:32.850
&gt;&gt; They just didn't
have the right,

01:04:32.851 --> 01:04:34.145
the question is what made

01:04:34.146 --> 01:04:36.295
certain primes as posted
on the mailing list bad.

01:04:36.296 --> 01:04:37.905
And it was that they didn't have

01:04:37.906 --> 01:04:41.410
the right structure for
these optimizations to work.

01:04:41.411 --> 01:04:44.670
&gt;&gt; Was it far away from
the power of two or something?

01:04:44.671 --> 01:04:47.560
&gt;&gt; Probably. I'm not sure.
Did you have a thought?

01:04:47.561 --> 01:04:48.793
&gt;&gt; That's what I wanted to say.

01:04:48.794 --> 01:04:50.240
&gt;&gt; Okay. And it's also the case

01:04:50.241 --> 01:04:52.078
that sometimes the same prime

01:04:52.079 --> 01:04:56.150
can be usefully expressed in
multiple different formulas.

01:04:56.151 --> 01:04:59.055
And one formula is
best for 64-bit,

01:04:59.056 --> 01:05:01.160
one formula is best for 32-bit.

01:05:01.161 --> 01:05:02.900
And so we had to use that to

01:05:02.901 --> 01:05:07.450
get the right behavior
in some cases.

01:05:13.200 --> 01:05:16.301
&gt;&gt; All right. Well,
let's thank Adam.

01:05:16.302 --> 01:05:17.370
&gt;&gt;Thanks.

